WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.036 --> 00:02.476 align:middle
April 28, 2021 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 1

00:02.546 --> 00:05.756 align:middle
of 29 Transcript of Chair
Powell's Press Conference April

00:05.756 --> 00:07.146 align:middle
28, 2021 CHAIR POWELL.

00:07.146 --> 00:07.656 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:07.656 --> 00:09.506 align:middle
At the Federal Reserve,
we are strongly committed

00:09.506 --> 00:10.926 align:middle
to achieving the
monetary policy goals

00:10.926 --> 00:11.886 align:middle
that Congress has given us:

00:11.886 --> 00:13.246 align:middle
maximum employment
and price stability.

00:13.246 --> 00:14.916 align:middle
Today my colleagues on the FOMC

00:14.916 --> 00:17.036 align:middle
and I kept interest
rates near zero

00:17.476 --> 00:20.066 align:middle
and maintained our
sizable asset purchases.

00:20.966 --> 00:23.596 align:middle
These measures, along
with our strong guidance

00:23.596 --> 00:25.776 align:middle
on interest rates and
on our balance sheet,

00:26.316 --> 00:28.726 align:middle
will ensure that monetary
policy will continue

00:28.726 --> 00:30.936 align:middle
to deliver powerful
support to the economy

00:31.286 --> 00:32.896 align:middle
until the recovery is complete.

00:35.236 --> 00:36.546 align:middle
Widespread vaccinations,

00:36.896 --> 00:39.666 align:middle
along with unprecedented
fiscal policy actions,

00:39.926 --> 00:42.626 align:middle
are also providing strong
support to the recovery.

00:43.586 --> 00:46.616 align:middle
Since the beginning of the year,
indicators of economic activity

00:46.616 --> 00:47.846 align:middle
and employment have
strengthened.

00:48.876 --> 00:51.586 align:middle
Household spending on
goods has risen robustly.

00:52.556 --> 00:54.686 align:middle
The housing sector has
more than fully recovered

00:54.686 --> 00:57.226 align:middle
from the downturn, while
business investment

00:57.226 --> 00:59.666 align:middle
and manufacturing production
have also increased.

01:00.976 --> 01:03.016 align:middle
Spending on services
has also picked up,

01:03.206 --> 01:05.006 align:middle
including at restaurants
and bars.

01:05.946 --> 01:07.326 align:middle
More generally, the sectors

01:07.326 --> 01:09.476 align:middle
of the economy most
adversely affected

01:09.476 --> 01:12.666 align:middle
by the pandemic remain weak
but have shown improvement.

01:13.826 --> 01:16.546 align:middle
While the recovery has
progressed more quickly

01:16.546 --> 01:18.986 align:middle
than generally expected,
it remains uneven

01:19.056 --> 01:20.176 align:middle
and far from complete.

01:20.716 --> 01:23.606 align:middle
The path of the economy
continues

01:23.606 --> 01:26.226 align:middle
to depend significantly
on the course of the virus

01:26.626 --> 01:29.106 align:middle
and the measures undertaken
to control its spread.

01:30.326 --> 01:31.526 align:middle
Since March, progress

01:31.526 --> 01:34.096 align:middle
on vaccinations has limited
the number of new cases,

01:34.266 --> 01:35.916 align:middle
hospitalizations, and deaths.

01:36.686 --> 01:39.086 align:middle
While the level of new
cases remains concerning,

01:39.246 --> 01:41.206 align:middle
especially as it
reflects the spread

01:41.206 --> 01:43.306 align:middle
of more infectious
strains of the virus,

01:43.986 --> 01:47.076 align:middle
continued vaccinations
should allow for a return

01:47.076 --> 01:49.306 align:middle
to more normal economic
conditions later this year.

01:49.306 --> 01:51.356 align:middle
In the April 28, 2021 Chair
Powell's Press Conference FINAL

01:51.356 --> 01:53.886 align:middle
Page 2 of 29 meantime, continued
observance of public health

01:53.886 --> 01:55.646 align:middle
and safety guidance
will help us reach

01:55.646 --> 01:57.466 align:middle
that goal as soon as possible.

01:57.976 --> 02:02.306 align:middle
As with overall economic
activity,

02:02.676 --> 02:05.306 align:middle
conditions in the labor market
have continued to improve.

02:06.266 --> 02:10.506 align:middle
Employment rose 916,000
in March, as the leisure

02:10.506 --> 02:13.076 align:middle
and hospitality sector
posted a notable gain

02:13.076 --> 02:14.536 align:middle
for the second consecutive
month.

02:15.736 --> 02:18.526 align:middle
Nonetheless, employment in
this sector is still more

02:18.526 --> 02:21.656 align:middle
than 3 million below its level
at the onset of the pandemic.

02:22.836 --> 02:24.006 align:middle
For the economy as a whole,

02:24.196 --> 02:27.206 align:middle
payroll employment is
8.4 million below its

02:27.206 --> 02:28.256 align:middle
pre-pandemic level.

02:29.626 --> 02:33.436 align:middle
The unemployment rate remained
elevated at 6 percent in March,

02:33.616 --> 02:36.306 align:middle
and this figure understates
the shortfall in employment,

02:36.866 --> 02:38.526 align:middle
particularly as participation

02:38.526 --> 02:40.696 align:middle
in the labor market
remains notably below

02:40.696 --> 02:41.966 align:middle
pre-pandemic levels.

02:42.416 --> 02:47.176 align:middle
The economic downturn has not
fallen equally on all Americans,

02:47.286 --> 02:50.046 align:middle
and those least able to shoulder
the burden have been the

02:50.046 --> 02:50.626 align:middle
hardest hit.

02:51.506 --> 02:52.976 align:middle
In particular, the high level

02:52.976 --> 02:55.196 align:middle
of joblessness has
been especially severe

02:55.196 --> 02:58.246 align:middle
for lower-wage workers
in the service sector

02:58.596 --> 03:00.646 align:middle
and for African Americans
and Hispanics.

03:00.726 --> 03:04.346 align:middle
The economic dislocation
has upended many lives

03:04.346 --> 03:06.566 align:middle
and created great
uncertainty about the future.

03:08.266 --> 03:10.386 align:middle
Readings on inflation have
increased and are likely

03:10.386 --> 03:12.516 align:middle
to rise somewhat further
before moderating.

03:13.336 --> 03:15.156 align:middle
In the near term,
12-month measures

03:15.156 --> 03:18.726 align:middle
of PCE inflation are expected
to move above 2 percent

03:19.186 --> 03:22.246 align:middle
as the very low readings from
early in the pandemic fall

03:22.246 --> 03:26.216 align:middle
out of the calculation and past
increases in oil prices pass

03:26.216 --> 03:28.376 align:middle
through to consumer
energy prices.

03:29.456 --> 03:31.216 align:middle
Beyond these effects,
we are also likely

03:31.216 --> 03:34.196 align:middle
to see upward pressure on prices
from the rebound in spending

03:34.196 --> 03:37.886 align:middle
as the economy continues
to reopen, particularly

03:37.886 --> 03:41.046 align:middle
if supply bottlenecks limit how
quickly production can respond

03:41.046 --> 03:41.806 align:middle
in the near term.

03:42.926 --> 03:45.836 align:middle
However, these one-time
increases in prices are likely

03:45.836 --> 03:47.116 align:middle
to have only transitory
effects on inflation.

03:47.116 --> 03:50.836 align:middle
April 28, 2021 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 3

03:50.836 --> 03:53.026 align:middle
of 29 The Fed's response to
this crisis has been guided

03:53.056 --> 03:55.246 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

03:55.246 --> 03:57.166 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people,

03:57.636 --> 04:00.576 align:middle
along with our responsibilities
to promote the stability

04:00.576 --> 04:01.566 align:middle
of the financial system.

04:02.556 --> 04:04.926 align:middle
As we say in our Statement
on Longer-Run Goals

04:04.926 --> 04:08.176 align:middle
and Monetary Policy Strategy,
we view maximum employment

04:08.176 --> 04:10.206 align:middle
as a broad-based
and inclusive goal.

04:11.106 --> 04:13.286 align:middle
Our ability to achieve
maximum employment

04:13.286 --> 04:15.006 align:middle
in the years ahead
depends importantly

04:15.206 --> 04:18.436 align:middle
on having longer-term inflation
expectations well anchored

04:18.436 --> 04:19.336 align:middle
at 2 percent.

04:19.566 --> 04:23.386 align:middle
As the Committee reiterated
in today's policy statement,

04:23.806 --> 04:26.326 align:middle
with inflation running
persistently below 2 percent,

04:26.856 --> 04:30.056 align:middle
we will aim to achieve inflation
moderately above 2 percent

04:30.056 --> 04:34.286 align:middle
for some time so that inflation
averages 2 percent over time

04:34.676 --> 04:37.536 align:middle
and longer-term inflation
expectations remain well

04:37.536 --> 04:38.976 align:middle
anchored at 2 percent.

04:40.236 --> 04:42.436 align:middle
We expect to maintain
an accommodative stance

04:42.436 --> 04:44.516 align:middle
of monetary policy
until these employment

04:44.516 --> 04:46.666 align:middle
and inflation outcomes
are achieved.

04:46.786 --> 04:49.326 align:middle
With regard to interest rates,

04:49.326 --> 04:51.356 align:middle
we continue to expect
it will be appropriate

04:51.356 --> 04:55.196 align:middle
to maintain the current 0
to ¼ percent target range

04:55.196 --> 04:56.206 align:middle
for the federal funds rate

04:56.836 --> 04:59.366 align:middle
until labor market conditions
have reached levels consistent

04:59.366 --> 05:01.376 align:middle
with the Committee's
assessment of maximum employment

05:02.036 --> 05:05.146 align:middle
and inflation has risen to
2 percent and is on track

05:05.146 --> 05:07.596 align:middle
to moderately exceed 2
percent for some time.

05:08.436 --> 05:10.736 align:middle
I would note that a
transitory rise in inflation

05:10.736 --> 05:13.916 align:middle
above 2 percent this year
would not meet this standard.

05:15.256 --> 05:17.996 align:middle
In addition, we will continue
to increase our holdings

05:17.996 --> 05:21.146 align:middle
of Treasury securities by at
least $80 billion per month

05:21.656 --> 05:23.886 align:middle
and of agency mortgage-backed
securities

05:23.926 --> 05:26.036 align:middle
by at least $40 billion
per month

05:26.726 --> 05:29.796 align:middle
until substantial further
progress has been made toward

05:29.796 --> 05:32.256 align:middle
our maximum-employment
and price-stability goals.

05:33.506 --> 05:34.836 align:middle
The increase in our
balance sheet

05:34.836 --> 05:38.486 align:middle
since March 2020 has materially
eased financial conditions

05:38.836 --> 05:41.356 align:middle
and is providing substantial
support to the economy.

05:41.926 --> 05:45.316 align:middle
The economy is a long way from
our goals, and it is likely

05:45.316 --> 05:46.476 align:middle
to take some time

05:46.556 --> 05:48.826 align:middle
for substantial further
progress to be achieved.

05:48.826 --> 05:51.196 align:middle
April 28, 2021 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 4

05:51.196 --> 05:52.866 align:middle
of 29 Our guidance
for interest rates

05:52.866 --> 05:54.666 align:middle
and asset purchases ties the
path of the federal funds rate

05:54.666 --> 05:55.916 align:middle
and the size of the
balance sheet

05:56.226 --> 05:57.986 align:middle
to our employment
and inflation goals.

05:58.656 --> 06:01.636 align:middle
This outcome-based guidance
will ensure that the stance

06:01.636 --> 06:03.986 align:middle
of monetary policy remains
highly accommodative

06:04.316 --> 06:05.706 align:middle
as the recovery progresses.

06:06.286 --> 06:10.756 align:middle
To conclude, we understand that
our actions affect communities,

06:10.756 --> 06:12.966 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

06:13.596 --> 06:16.096 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

06:16.986 --> 06:19.926 align:middle
We at the Fed will do everything
we can to support the economy

06:20.006 --> 06:22.306 align:middle
for as long as it takes
to complete the recovery.

06:22.956 --> 06:23.456 align:middle
Thank you.

06:23.456 --> 06:24.666 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

06:26.706 --> 06:27.296 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

06:27.566 --> 06:28.576 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chair.

06:28.576 --> 06:30.086 align:middle
First we'll go Paul Kiernan.

06:32.396 --> 06:32.756 align:middle
PAUL KIERNAN.

06:32.756 --> 06:33.946 align:middle
Hi, Chairman Powell.

06:33.946 --> 06:35.476 align:middle
Thanks for doing this.

06:35.476 --> 06:38.186 align:middle
I guess, since I'm
first, I'll go ahead

06:38.186 --> 06:42.006 align:middle
and re-up Howard's question
from the last press conference:

06:42.006 --> 06:43.356 align:middle
Is it time to start talking

06:43.356 --> 06:45.206 align:middle
about talking about
tapering yet?

06:45.206 --> 06:48.086 align:middle
Have you and your colleagues
had any conversations

06:48.086 --> 06:48.796 align:middle
to this effect?

06:48.876 --> 06:50.466 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

06:50.656 --> 06:51.096 align:middle
Thank you.

06:51.736 --> 06:53.146 align:middle
So, no, it is not time yet.

06:54.026 --> 06:56.376 align:middle
We've said that we would let
the public know when it is time

06:56.376 --> 06:59.246 align:middle
to have that conversation,
and we said we'd do that well

06:59.246 --> 07:00.796 align:middle
in advance of any
actual decision

07:00.796 --> 07:03.106 align:middle
to taper our asset
purchases, and we will do so.

07:03.106 --> 07:05.646 align:middle
In the meantime, we'll be
monitoring progress toward

07:05.646 --> 07:06.616 align:middle
our goals.

07:07.256 --> 07:08.576 align:middle
We first articulated this

07:08.576 --> 07:11.496 align:middle
"substantial further progress"
test at our December meeting.

07:11.876 --> 07:14.516 align:middle
Economic activity and hiring
have just recently picked

07:14.516 --> 07:15.916 align:middle
up after slowing
over the winter.

07:16.326 --> 07:19.586 align:middle
And it will take some time
before we see substantial

07:19.586 --> 07:20.306 align:middle
further progress.

07:20.406 --> 07:20.916 align:middle
PAUL KIERNAN.

07:22.386 --> 07:22.986 align:middle
Thank you.

07:22.986 --> 07:23.846 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:24.526 --> 07:25.436 align:middle
Thank you.

07:25.436 --> 07:26.516 align:middle
Jonnelle at Reuters.

07:26.516 --> 07:32.306 align:middle
April 28, 2021 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 5

07:32.356 --> 07:33.166 align:middle
of 29 JONNELLE MARTE.

07:33.166 --> 07:33.756 align:middle
Okay. Hi. Thank you so much.

07:33.756 --> 07:36.836 align:middle
My question is about what's
going on with the virus,

07:36.836 --> 07:41.646 align:middle
and what if we don't reach a
level of herd immunity, per se.

07:41.646 --> 07:44.096 align:middle
So, in your scenario,
thinking about the outlook

07:44.096 --> 07:47.926 align:middle
and the economy, do you have
some model where you might start

07:47.926 --> 07:51.556 align:middle
to still normalize policy even
if there's like a baseline

07:51.556 --> 07:53.366 align:middle
of infection still going on?

07:53.366 --> 07:54.516 align:middle
Or how can you, you know,

07:54.516 --> 07:57.356 align:middle
talk about how you're
weighing those things?

07:57.356 --> 07:59.206 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:59.206 --> 08:01.986 align:middle
Yes, so we have to leave
questions about herd immunity

08:01.986 --> 08:04.766 align:middle
and what that looks like
to the health experts, but,

08:04.766 --> 08:08.586 align:middle
but I would certainly say
that what matters the most

08:08.586 --> 08:10.016 align:middle
to the economic recovery
continues

08:10.016 --> 08:11.316 align:middle
to be controlling the virus.

08:11.716 --> 08:14.976 align:middle
The economy can't fully recover
until people are confident

08:14.976 --> 08:17.366 align:middle
that it's safe to resume
activities involving crowds

08:17.366 --> 08:17.856 align:middle
of people.

08:18.366 --> 08:20.166 align:middle
There may be people who
are around the edges

08:20.166 --> 08:21.886 align:middle
of the labor force
who won't come back

08:21.886 --> 08:25.246 align:middle
in unless they feel really
comfortable going back

08:25.246 --> 08:28.146 align:middle
to their old jobs, for example,
and there, there may be parts

08:28.146 --> 08:31.566 align:middle
of the economy that will-that
just won't be able to,

08:32.246 --> 08:33.996 align:middle
to really fully reengage

08:33.996 --> 08:38.276 align:middle
until the pandemic is
decisively behind us.

08:38.736 --> 08:44.056 align:middle
But, you know, we've
articulated particular guidance

08:44.056 --> 08:47.296 align:middle
for tapering our asset
purchases and for lifting off

08:47.336 --> 08:48.346 align:middle
and raising interest rates.

08:48.346 --> 08:50.066 align:middle
So for, for asset purchases,

08:50.066 --> 08:52.546 align:middle
we've said that we would
continue at the current pace

08:52.636 --> 08:56.846 align:middle
of asset purchases until we see
substantial further progress

08:56.996 --> 08:57.846 align:middle
toward our goals.

08:58.356 --> 09:01.056 align:middle
So-and that is, that
is what it is,

09:01.056 --> 09:02.386 align:middle
substantial further progress.

09:03.066 --> 09:05.996 align:middle
For interest rates, as
I-as I said a moment ago,

09:05.996 --> 09:09.216 align:middle
we want to see labor
market conditions consistent

09:09.216 --> 09:12.686 align:middle
with maximum employment, we want
to see inflation at 2 percent,

09:12.986 --> 09:14.836 align:middle
and we want to see it on
track to exceed 2 percent.

09:14.836 --> 09:15.756 align:middle
So those are our tests.

09:15.756 --> 09:18.946 align:middle
We don't have an independent
test to do with the virus.

09:18.946 --> 09:21.976 align:middle
I will say, though, as I-as I
started with, you know, the,

09:21.976 --> 09:25.146 align:middle
the path of the virus is going
to have an effect on our ability

09:25.146 --> 09:27.416 align:middle
to achieve both of those tests.

09:27.786 --> 09:29.626 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

09:29.796 --> 09:32.996 align:middle
Great. Thank you.

09:33.206 --> 09:38.866 align:middle
Jeanna. April 28, 2021 Chair
Powell's Press Conference FINAL

09:38.866 --> 09:40.176 align:middle
Page 6 of 29 JEANNA SMIALEK.

09:40.176 --> 09:40.676 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

09:40.676 --> 09:41.726 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

09:41.726 --> 09:43.976 align:middle
I wonder if you could talk
a little bit about that test

09:43.976 --> 09:46.556 align:middle
for raising interest rates
that you just elaborated.

09:46.556 --> 09:48.366 align:middle
You've obviously made it
very clear that you want

09:48.366 --> 09:50.966 align:middle
to see improvements in the
real economy and the real data

09:50.966 --> 09:52.496 align:middle
and not in just sort

09:52.496 --> 09:55.126 align:middle
of expectations data
before making that move.

09:55.126 --> 09:56.876 align:middle
But I guess I wonder
what happens

09:56.876 --> 09:59.576 align:middle
if inflation expectations
were to move

09:59.576 --> 10:03.316 align:middle
up before you see some sort
of return to full employment.

10:03.316 --> 10:05.846 align:middle
You know, it seems like a
lot of sort of the stability

10:05.846 --> 10:08.176 align:middle
in inflation has been tied to
the fact that those have been

10:08.176 --> 10:10.636 align:middle
so low and stable, and
I guess I wonder how,

10:10.636 --> 10:13.806 align:middle
how your reaction would be-sort
of how your reaction to that is,

10:13.806 --> 10:15.286 align:middle
is-how you're thinking
about that.

10:16.566 --> 10:16.966 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:17.066 --> 10:21.186 align:middle
Right. So it seems
unlikely, frankly,

10:21.656 --> 10:25.906 align:middle
that we would see inflation
moving up in a persistent way

10:25.906 --> 10:27.946 align:middle
that would actually move
inflation expectations

10:27.946 --> 10:30.006 align:middle
up while there was
still significant slack

10:30.006 --> 10:30.806 align:middle
in the labor market.

10:30.876 --> 10:33.436 align:middle
I won't say that it's
impossible, but it,

10:33.436 --> 10:35.556 align:middle
it seems-it seems unlikely.

10:36.886 --> 10:40.736 align:middle
It's much more likely that
we would-having achieved

10:40.826 --> 10:45.096 align:middle
maximum-employment
conditions-we'd also be seeing 2

10:45.096 --> 10:46.656 align:middle
percent inflation
and be on track

10:46.656 --> 10:48.616 align:middle
to see inflation
moving above 2 percent.

10:48.616 --> 10:49.796 align:middle
They tend to move together.

10:50.516 --> 10:55.116 align:middle
So that's not to say inflation
won't-might not move up,

10:55.356 --> 10:58.416 align:middle
but for inflation to move
up in a persistent way that,

10:58.546 --> 11:01.486 align:middle
that really starts to move
inflation expectations up,

11:01.886 --> 11:03.946 align:middle
that would have to-that
would take some time.

11:04.566 --> 11:07.166 align:middle
And you would think that, that
it would be quite likely that,

11:07.266 --> 11:09.626 align:middle
that we'd be in very
strong labor markets

11:09.676 --> 11:10.736 align:middle
for that to be happening.

11:10.776 --> 11:13.236 align:middle
If it-if that actually
were to happen, though,

11:13.236 --> 11:16.496 align:middle
there is a-there's a paragraph
in the-in the Statement

11:16.496 --> 11:19.266 align:middle
on Longer-Run Goals and Monetary
Policy Strategy which says

11:19.266 --> 11:21.906 align:middle
that when the two goals
are somewhat in conflict,

11:21.906 --> 11:24.596 align:middle
we weigh various
factors, including the,

11:24.596 --> 11:26.236 align:middle
the time it would take to
get back and [so] forth.

11:26.236 --> 11:28.606 align:middle
So we do-it doesn't really tell
you what to do, but it tells you

11:28.606 --> 11:31.816 align:middle
that we will weigh those two
factors and how far we are away

11:31.816 --> 11:34.046 align:middle
from them and how long it would
take to reach them were we

11:34.046 --> 11:36.136 align:middle
to reach that sort of
pretty unlikely state.

11:36.136 --> 11:41.406 align:middle
April 28, 2021 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 7

11:41.406 --> 11:42.106 align:middle
of 29 MICHELLE SMITH.

11:42.106 --> 11:42.286 align:middle
Thank you.

11:42.286 --> 11:42.636 align:middle
Steve Liesman.

11:42.636 --> 11:43.076 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

11:43.076 --> 11:43.296 align:middle
Thank you.

11:43.296 --> 11:45.386 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, I want to follow
up on Jonnelle's question,

11:45.386 --> 11:48.646 align:middle
and I wonder if you can help us
understand better your thinking

11:48.646 --> 11:51.186 align:middle
about how COVID infection rates

11:51.186 --> 11:53.756 align:middle
and the virus calibrate
with monetary policy.

11:53.756 --> 11:56.446 align:middle
As you recall, we had
a major resurgence

11:56.446 --> 11:58.516 align:middle
in the virus last fall.

11:58.516 --> 12:00.956 align:middle
And I'm wondering, would
you need to be assured

12:00.956 --> 12:01.736 align:middle
that there wasn't

12:01.736 --> 12:04.956 align:middle
such a resurgence coming
before you began-I mean,

12:04.956 --> 12:06.316 align:middle
for lack of a better
term-thinking

12:06.316 --> 12:08.436 align:middle
about thinking about tapering?

12:08.436 --> 12:10.986 align:middle
And what kind of comfort
level would you need?

12:10.986 --> 12:14.996 align:middle
Would it be something that would
be declared from the CDC or the,

12:14.996 --> 12:17.246 align:middle
or the World Health
Organization-for example,

12:17.246 --> 12:19.476 align:middle
downgrading the virus

12:19.476 --> 12:22.506 align:middle
from a pandemic-before
you have the comfort level

12:22.506 --> 12:23.996 align:middle
to reverse course on policy?

12:23.996 --> 12:24.616 align:middle
Thank you.

12:24.616 --> 12:25.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:25.556 --> 12:28.036 align:middle
Yes. We, we really have-just
articulated the goals

12:28.036 --> 12:29.916 align:middle
that I've-that I've just
mentioned a couple of times,

12:29.916 --> 12:33.176 align:middle
which is substantial further
progress toward our goals before

12:33.176 --> 12:33.746 align:middle
we taper.

12:34.036 --> 12:36.906 align:middle
Now, that's very likely-for
us to achieve that,

12:36.906 --> 12:38.826 align:middle
it's very likely
going to be the case

12:39.346 --> 12:41.976 align:middle
that we've also made
really significant progress

12:41.976 --> 12:45.656 align:middle
on controlling the virus through
vaccination and, and other-it,

12:45.656 --> 12:48.276 align:middle
it-you know, those two things
should more or less coexist.

12:48.556 --> 12:51.206 align:middle
We haven't articulated a
separate test for a state

12:51.206 --> 12:52.796 align:middle
of the virus that
we'd like to achieve

12:53.486 --> 12:56.556 align:middle
because we're not-you know,
we're not experts in that area.

12:56.556 --> 13:00.376 align:middle
We're, we're really focused
on the economic outcomes, and,

13:00.376 --> 13:03.656 align:middle
again, my, my guess is
that we-it's very likely,

13:03.656 --> 13:06.276 align:middle
it seems to me, that for us to
achieve the economic outcomes,

13:06.276 --> 13:09.006 align:middle
we would need to taper or
to raise interest rates.

13:09.366 --> 13:12.256 align:middle
We would also have to have
made very substantial progress

13:12.336 --> 13:14.926 align:middle
in getting the virus
under control-not,

13:15.066 --> 13:16.806 align:middle
not necessarily fully
under control.

13:16.806 --> 13:19.486 align:middle
There is a, a possibility,
of course, that we will,

13:19.486 --> 13:21.076 align:middle
will have ongoing
outbreaks over the summer

13:21.076 --> 13:22.436 align:middle
and various regional outbreaks

13:22.436 --> 13:24.666 align:middle
and potentially next
winter April 28,

13:24.666 --> 13:27.066 align:middle
2021 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 8

13:27.066 --> 13:27.606 align:middle
of 29 as well.

13:27.606 --> 13:28.266 align:middle
But we'll be looking

13:28.266 --> 13:31.286 align:middle
for substantial progress toward
our goal-substantial further

13:31.286 --> 13:32.856 align:middle
progress toward our
goals as we think

13:32.856 --> 13:34.206 align:middle
about tapering asset purchases.

13:34.206 --> 13:34.866 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

13:36.096 --> 13:36.416 align:middle
Thank you.

13:36.416 --> 13:37.176 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

13:37.476 --> 13:37.986 align:middle
Thank you.

13:37.986 --> 13:42.626 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

13:42.736 --> 13:43.496 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

13:43.496 --> 13:47.186 align:middle
I wanted to ask, you've talked
a lot during this crisis

13:47.186 --> 13:49.926 align:middle
about the need to have a lot
of help from fiscal policy

13:49.926 --> 13:51.326 align:middle
and monetary policy to make sure

13:51.326 --> 13:54.076 align:middle
that there's not long-term
scarring in the economy.

13:54.076 --> 13:56.416 align:middle
And I was just wondering, you
know, given all of the policies

13:56.416 --> 13:58.686 align:middle
that have been put in
place, do you expect there

13:58.686 --> 14:01.086 align:middle
to still be some
long-term scarring?

14:01.086 --> 14:04.266 align:middle
And, if so, you know, what
are you most worried about?

14:04.546 --> 14:06.486 align:middle
Where are you most worried
about that showing up?

14:07.266 --> 14:07.686 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:07.686 --> 14:13.096 align:middle
So I would say that we were
very worried about scarring,

14:13.736 --> 14:16.716 align:middle
both in the labor market
from people being out of-out

14:16.716 --> 14:18.656 align:middle
of the labor market for an
extended period of time.

14:18.656 --> 14:21.166 align:middle
The evidence is clear that it
becomes much more difficult

14:21.166 --> 14:23.956 align:middle
if you're out for a long time
to get back in and get back

14:23.956 --> 14:24.846 align:middle
to the life that you had.

14:25.066 --> 14:26.606 align:middle
Same thing with these
small businesses,

14:26.606 --> 14:28.886 align:middle
many of which are the
work of generations.

14:29.386 --> 14:32.586 align:middle
Were we going to wipe out
many of those unnecessarily?

14:33.246 --> 14:34.326 align:middle
That was a big concern.

14:34.326 --> 14:36.856 align:middle
I would say that,
so far, you know,

14:36.856 --> 14:39.316 align:middle
here we are in late
April of 2021.

14:40.016 --> 14:44.136 align:middle
We haven't experienced that
level of scarring either

14:44.136 --> 14:46.286 align:middle
in the labor market or
among smaller businesses.

14:46.286 --> 14:48.306 align:middle
So we're not living
that, that downside case

14:48.306 --> 14:50.196 align:middle
that we were very
concerned about a year ago.

14:50.836 --> 14:54.706 align:middle
Notwithstanding that, we're a
long way from full employment.

14:54.706 --> 14:57.696 align:middle
We're-you know, payroll
jobs are 8.4 million below

14:57.696 --> 14:59.576 align:middle
where they were in
February of 2020.

14:59.576 --> 15:00.436 align:middle
We've got a long ways to go.

15:00.436 --> 15:02.006 align:middle
And also, it's going to
be a different economy.

15:02.006 --> 15:04.846 align:middle
April 28, 2021 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 9

15:04.846 --> 15:07.166 align:middle
of 29 So we've been
hearing a lot from companies

15:07.166 --> 15:08.466 align:middle
that they are-they've
been looking

15:08.466 --> 15:11.246 align:middle
at deploying better technology
and perhaps fewer people,

15:11.246 --> 15:13.006 align:middle
including in some of
the service industries

15:13.006 --> 15:14.446 align:middle
that have been employing
a lot of people.

15:15.076 --> 15:19.156 align:middle
You know, it, it may well be-it
seems quite likely that a number

15:19.156 --> 15:21.856 align:middle
of the people who, who had
those service-sector jobs will

15:21.856 --> 15:26.036 align:middle
struggle to find the same job
and may need time to find work

15:26.036 --> 15:28.386 align:middle
and get back to the
working life they had.

15:28.386 --> 15:31.016 align:middle
These were people who were
working in February of 2020.

15:31.016 --> 15:32.096 align:middle
They clearly want to work.

15:32.636 --> 15:35.176 align:middle
So those people are going to
need-they're going to need help.

15:35.286 --> 15:40.366 align:middle
And so, while I would say we
haven't seen really highly

15:40.366 --> 15:44.316 align:middle
elevated levels of unemployment
for-you know, up in the teens

15:44.316 --> 15:46.536 align:middle
that we thought we might have
for an extended period of time,

15:46.536 --> 15:48.746 align:middle
we've still got a lot of people
who are out to-out of work.

15:48.746 --> 15:51.226 align:middle
We want to get them back to
work as quickly as possible,

15:51.226 --> 15:53.466 align:middle
and that's really one of
the things we're trying

15:53.466 --> 15:54.576 align:middle
to achieve with our policy.

15:54.576 --> 15:55.196 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

15:57.736 --> 15:58.506 align:middle
Thank you.

15:58.506 --> 15:59.026 align:middle
Rachel Siegel.

15:59.026 --> 15:59.546 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

16:01.946 --> 16:03.686 align:middle
Thank you, Michelle, and
thank you, Chair Powell,

16:03.686 --> 16:05.206 align:middle
for taking our questions.

16:05.426 --> 16:08.636 align:middle
The housing market in many
American cities is seeing

16:08.636 --> 16:10.906 align:middle
booming prices, bidding wars,

16:10.906 --> 16:13.356 align:middle
and all-cash offers
well above asking price.

16:13.356 --> 16:15.436 align:middle
And this is happening
at the same time

16:15.436 --> 16:17.366 align:middle
that housing is becoming
much more expensive

16:17.366 --> 16:19.576 align:middle
for lower-income
Americans and people

16:19.576 --> 16:21.456 align:middle
who are still struggling
from the pandemic.

16:21.806 --> 16:24.506 align:middle
Do you have concerns that there
are localized housing bubbles

16:24.506 --> 16:26.376 align:middle
or that there's the
potential for that,

16:26.376 --> 16:29.246 align:middle
and what is the Fed doing
to monitor or address this?

16:29.246 --> 16:29.866 align:middle
Thank you.

16:29.866 --> 16:30.126 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:30.126 --> 16:34.276 align:middle
So we do monitor the housing
market very carefully,

16:34.276 --> 16:34.846 align:middle
of course.

16:34.846 --> 16:38.186 align:middle
And I would say that,
before the pandemic,

16:38.186 --> 16:41.916 align:middle
it's a very different
housing market

16:41.916 --> 16:44.826 align:middle
than it was before the
Global Financial Crisis.

16:44.826 --> 16:47.666 align:middle
And one of the main differences
was that households were

16:47.666 --> 16:48.616 align:middle
in very good April 28,

16:48.616 --> 16:50.756 align:middle
2021 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 10

16:50.836 --> 16:52.936 align:middle
of 29 shape financially
compared to where they were.

16:52.936 --> 16:54.966 align:middle
In addition, most people who
got mortgages were people

16:54.966 --> 16:56.176 align:middle
with pretty high credit scores.

16:56.176 --> 17:00.976 align:middle
There wasn't the
subprime-you know, low-doc,

17:00.976 --> 17:03.406 align:middle
no-doc lending practices
were not there.

17:03.406 --> 17:05.296 align:middle
So we don't have
that kind of thing

17:05.296 --> 17:08.226 align:middle
where we have a housing bubble
where people are overlevered

17:08.226 --> 17:09.186 align:middle
and owning a lot of houses.

17:09.466 --> 17:10.326 align:middle
There's no question, though,

17:10.326 --> 17:11.786 align:middle
that housing prices
are going up,

17:11.786 --> 17:14.136 align:middle
and so we're watching
that carefully.

17:14.136 --> 17:16.646 align:middle
It's, it's partly
because there's,

17:16.646 --> 17:19.216 align:middle
there's clearly strong demand,

17:19.216 --> 17:21.356 align:middle
and there's just not a
lot of supply right now.

17:21.356 --> 17:23.786 align:middle
So builders are, you
know, struggling to keep

17:23.786 --> 17:24.956 align:middle
up with the demand, clearly.

17:24.956 --> 17:26.496 align:middle
Inventories are tremendously
low.

17:26.496 --> 17:28.026 align:middle
We're, we're all
hearing those stories.

17:28.366 --> 17:31.116 align:middle
And if you're-if you're an
entry level housing buyer,

17:31.116 --> 17:35.266 align:middle
this is-this is a problem,
because it just is going to be

17:35.266 --> 17:36.576 align:middle
that much harder
for people to get

17:36.576 --> 17:38.666 align:middle
that first house,
and that's a problem.

17:39.106 --> 17:42.706 align:middle
I mean, we-it's part
of a strong economy,

17:42.706 --> 17:44.166 align:middle
with people having
money to spend

17:44.166 --> 17:46.026 align:middle
and wanting to invest
in housing.

17:46.456 --> 17:47.616 align:middle
So, in that sense, it's good.

17:47.616 --> 17:50.396 align:middle
It's clearly the strongest
housing market that we've seen

17:50.396 --> 17:51.986 align:middle
since the Global
Financial Crisis.

17:52.676 --> 17:54.906 align:middle
And, you know, my hope
would be that, over time,

17:54.906 --> 17:57.446 align:middle
housing builders can
react to this demand and,

17:57.446 --> 18:00.366 align:middle
and come up with more supply
and workers will come back

18:00.366 --> 18:01.366 align:middle
to work in that industry.

18:01.366 --> 18:05.796 align:middle
So, you know, it is-it's
not an unalloyed good

18:05.796 --> 18:07.226 align:middle
to have prices going
up this much,

18:07.226 --> 18:08.626 align:middle
and we're watching
it very carefully.

18:09.256 --> 18:10.706 align:middle
I don't see the kind

18:10.706 --> 18:12.936 align:middle
of financial stability
current concerns, though,

18:14.016 --> 18:17.996 align:middle
that really do reside
around the housing sector.

18:17.996 --> 18:20.676 align:middle
So many of the financial
crackups in all countries,

18:20.676 --> 18:22.096 align:middle
all western countries,
that have happened

18:22.546 --> 18:24.406 align:middle
in the last 30 years
have been around housing.

18:24.406 --> 18:25.996 align:middle
We don't-we really
don't see that here.

18:25.996 --> 18:28.316 align:middle
We don't see bad loans
and unsustainable prices

18:28.316 --> 18:29.006 align:middle
and that kind of thing.

18:29.006 --> 18:29.666 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

18:32.606 --> 18:33.046 align:middle
Thank you.

18:33.046 --> 18:34.496 align:middle
Chris Rugaber, Associated Press.

18:34.496 --> 18:39.266 align:middle
April 28, 2021 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 11

18:39.366 --> 18:40.326 align:middle
of 29 CHRIS RUGABER.

18:40.326 --> 18:41.856 align:middle
Thanks for taking my question.

18:41.856 --> 18:43.446 align:middle
I wanted to ask about
the labor market.

18:43.446 --> 18:46.376 align:middle
Do you-do you see things, such
as in the Beige Book-you saw

18:46.376 --> 18:49.126 align:middle
in the Beige Book anecdotes
about businesses not able

18:49.126 --> 18:56.476 align:middle
to find workers, [inaudible]
back to the labor market

18:56.476 --> 18:58.906 align:middle
for fear of getting sick,

18:58.906 --> 19:00.746 align:middle
childcare concerns,
and so forth.

19:00.746 --> 19:03.646 align:middle
Do you see these as also just
sort of temporary bottlenecks

19:03.646 --> 19:05.796 align:middle
that won't have much effect

19:05.796 --> 19:07.776 align:middle
on the long-term health
of the labor market?

19:07.776 --> 19:09.976 align:middle
Or how are you thinking
about these kinds of issues?

19:10.416 --> 19:11.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:11.506 --> 19:12.696 align:middle
Chris, you were breaking
up a little there,

19:12.696 --> 19:14.356 align:middle
but I think I did
get your question.

19:14.356 --> 19:18.956 align:middle
So I think there are a number
of-a number of things going

19:18.956 --> 19:23.166 align:middle
on there: the tension between a
high level of unemployment and

19:23.196 --> 19:29.166 align:middle
yet many, many companies
saying they can't find-

19:29.166 --> 19:30.346 align:middle
an elevated number
of companies saying

19:30.346 --> 19:31.386 align:middle
that they can't find workers.

19:31.386 --> 19:32.356 align:middle
So, what's really
going on there?

19:32.356 --> 19:34.676 align:middle
It, it should be a number
of different things.

19:35.126 --> 19:37.166 align:middle
There are workers who don't
actually have the specific

19:37.166 --> 19:38.886 align:middle
skills that the employers
are looking for.

19:39.226 --> 19:41.826 align:middle
There may be geographical
differences.

19:42.096 --> 19:44.866 align:middle
It, it may also be
that, for example,

19:44.866 --> 19:48.136 align:middle
there-one big factor would
be, schools aren't open yet,

19:48.136 --> 19:50.026 align:middle
so there are still people
who are at home taking care

19:50.026 --> 19:51.376 align:middle
of their children and
would like to be back

19:51.376 --> 19:52.956 align:middle
in the workforce
but can't be yet.

19:53.336 --> 19:55.806 align:middle
There are people afraid
of-there are virus fears

19:55.806 --> 19:56.706 align:middle
that are weighing on people,

19:56.706 --> 19:58.656 align:middle
so some-some people don't
want to go back to work.

19:59.256 --> 20:01.966 align:middle
There are also a
significant number of people

20:01.966 --> 20:04.796 align:middle
who say they've retired-a
large number of people

20:04.796 --> 20:05.826 align:middle
that say that they're retired.

20:05.826 --> 20:09.036 align:middle
Now, it's hard to say
whether they will come back

20:09.036 --> 20:12.266 align:middle
in as the labor market
strengthens and as COVID,

20:13.286 --> 20:15.846 align:middle
you know, becomes a-in
the rearview mirror,

20:15.846 --> 20:17.016 align:middle
in the history books,
if you will.

20:17.586 --> 20:22.716 align:middle
So-but clearly there's something
going, going on out there, as,

20:23.086 --> 20:26.006 align:middle
as many companies are
reporting labor shortages.

20:26.586 --> 20:27.786 align:middle
We don't see wages
moving up yet,

20:27.786 --> 20:29.226 align:middle
and presumably we
would see April 28,

20:29.226 --> 20:32.016 align:middle
2021 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 12 of 29

20:32.016 --> 20:33.916 align:middle
that in a real-you know, in
a really tight labor market.

20:34.056 --> 20:35.796 align:middle
And we may well start
to see that.

20:36.446 --> 20:42.026 align:middle
I do think-so what will
happen-what we saw during the

20:42.026 --> 20:44.516 align:middle
last expansion, and it may
be a different expansion,

20:44.596 --> 20:46.386 align:middle
we don't know, but,
but what we saw was

20:46.386 --> 20:48.746 align:middle
that labor supply
generally showed up.

20:48.746 --> 20:50.636 align:middle
In other words, if
you were worried

20:50.636 --> 20:52.126 align:middle
about there-about
running out of workers,

20:52.126 --> 20:53.476 align:middle
it seemed like we never did.

20:53.476 --> 20:55.816 align:middle
You know, labor force
participation held up.

20:56.216 --> 20:57.436 align:middle
People came in the labor force.

20:57.436 --> 20:59.716 align:middle
They stayed in the labor
force longer than expected.

21:00.306 --> 21:05.066 align:middle
So my guess would be that you
will see people coming back

21:05.066 --> 21:08.256 align:middle
into the labor force, and these
jobs will be-the labor market

21:08.256 --> 21:10.486 align:middle
will reach equilibrium.

21:10.686 --> 21:12.216 align:middle
Maybe pay will go up.

21:12.756 --> 21:16.456 align:middle
But I do think that-and
I do think also

21:16.456 --> 21:18.306 align:middle
that unemployment
insurance benefits will run

21:18.306 --> 21:20.476 align:middle
out in September, so to
the extent that's a factor,

21:20.476 --> 21:24.826 align:middle
which is not clear, it will no
longer be a factor fairly soon.

21:25.366 --> 21:28.326 align:middle
So my guess is, it will
come back to this economy

21:28.326 --> 21:29.806 align:middle
where we have, you know,

21:29.806 --> 21:32.876 align:middle
equilibrium between labor
supply and labor demand.

21:33.206 --> 21:34.366 align:middle
It may take some months, though.

21:34.366 --> 21:34.986 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

21:37.506 --> 21:38.426 align:middle
Thank you.

21:38.426 --> 21:38.756 align:middle
Heather Scott.

21:38.756 --> 21:39.196 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

21:39.196 --> 21:40.446 align:middle
Could I do a quick follow-up?

21:40.446 --> 21:40.716 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

21:40.716 --> 21:41.026 align:middle
I'm sorry.

21:41.026 --> 21:43.186 align:middle
Go ahead. Go ahead, Chris.

21:43.186 --> 21:43.576 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

21:44.536 --> 21:56.446 align:middle
Well [extended pause]
MICHELLE SMITH.

21:56.446 --> 21:57.906 align:middle
We, we lost you, Chris.

21:57.906 --> 21:58.736 align:middle
Let's go on to Heather.

21:58.736 --> 22:00.976 align:middle
HEATHER SCOTT.

22:00.976 --> 22:02.226 align:middle
Thank you.

22:02.226 --> 22:03.456 align:middle
Good afternoon, Chair Powell.

22:03.456 --> 22:07.166 align:middle
I wanted to ask you, you know,
you've been hearing, of course,

22:07.166 --> 22:10.106 align:middle
these concerns raised by
Larry Summers and others

22:10.106 --> 22:12.786 align:middle
about inflation and the fact

22:13.046 --> 22:16.246 align:middle
that they think the Fed
might be-might let things get

22:16.246 --> 22:19.236 align:middle
out of hand with the
new policy stance.

22:19.236 --> 22:22.706 align:middle
So my question is, can you tell
us, what is different this time

22:22.946 --> 22:25.386 align:middle
versus previous periods
like in the '60s

22:25.386 --> 22:26.806 align:middle
when inflation got
out of control?

22:26.806 --> 22:28.776 align:middle
Why are you confident
with, April 28,

22:28.776 --> 22:31.906 align:middle
2021 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 13 of 29

22:31.906 --> 22:33.916 align:middle
with the lags in monetary policy
that the Fed can get ahead

22:33.916 --> 22:35.776 align:middle
of inflation and, and make
sure it doesn't go too far

22:35.776 --> 22:38.026 align:middle
above the 2 percent target?

22:38.076 --> 22:38.426 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:38.426 --> 22:40.156 align:middle
Thanks for your question.

22:40.156 --> 22:41.756 align:middle
So I actually have a couple
things I'd like to say

22:41.756 --> 22:43.886 align:middle
about inflation, including
addressing your question.

22:44.456 --> 22:46.206 align:middle
So let me start with just saying

22:46.206 --> 22:47.686 align:middle
that we're very strongly
committed

22:47.686 --> 22:48.876 align:middle
to achieving our objectives

22:48.876 --> 22:51.196 align:middle
of maximum employment
and price stability.

22:51.936 --> 22:54.876 align:middle
Our, our price stability
goal is 2 percent inflation

22:54.876 --> 22:56.716 align:middle
over the longer run,
and we believe

22:56.716 --> 22:58.736 align:middle
that having inflation
average 2 percent

22:58.736 --> 23:01.326 align:middle
over time will help anchor
long-term inflation expectations

23:01.326 --> 23:01.976 align:middle
at 2 percent.

23:02.716 --> 23:05.226 align:middle
With inflation having run
persistently below 2 percent

23:05.226 --> 23:07.586 align:middle
for some time, the Committee
seeks inflation moderately

23:07.586 --> 23:11.446 align:middle
above inflation for two
times-above 2 percent

23:11.446 --> 23:12.026 align:middle
for some time.

23:12.846 --> 23:15.396 align:middle
So, with a, a little
bit of context, we're,

23:15.396 --> 23:18.366 align:middle
we're making our way through an
unprecedented series of events,

23:18.366 --> 23:22.756 align:middle
really, in which a synchronized
global shutdown is now giving

23:22.756 --> 23:24.926 align:middle
way to widespread
reopening of economies

23:24.926 --> 23:26.346 align:middle
in many places around the world.

23:26.686 --> 23:29.026 align:middle
In the United States, fiscal
and monetary policy continue

23:29.026 --> 23:32.336 align:middle
to provide strong support:
Vaccinations are now widespread,

23:32.336 --> 23:35.876 align:middle
and the economy is beginning to
move ahead with real momentum.

23:36.486 --> 23:39.226 align:middle
During this time of
reopening, we are likely

23:39.226 --> 23:42.286 align:middle
to see some upward pressure on
prices, and I'll discuss why.

23:42.786 --> 23:44.876 align:middle
But those pressures are
likely to be temporary

23:44.876 --> 23:47.606 align:middle
as they are associated
with the reopening process.

23:48.186 --> 23:50.426 align:middle
In an episode of
one-time price increases

23:50.426 --> 23:53.216 align:middle
as the economy reopens
is not the same thing as,

23:53.216 --> 23:54.786 align:middle
and is not likely to lead to,

23:55.116 --> 23:58.476 align:middle
persistently higher
year-over-year inflation

23:58.476 --> 24:01.426 align:middle
into the future- inflation at
levels that are not consistent

24:01.426 --> 24:04.336 align:middle
with our goal of 2 percent
inflation over time.

24:04.406 --> 24:06.606 align:middle
Indeed, it is the
Fed's job to make sure

24:06.606 --> 24:07.766 align:middle
that that does not happen.

24:08.446 --> 24:11.376 align:middle
If, if, contrary to
expectations, inflation were

24:11.376 --> 24:13.896 align:middle
to move persistently and
materially above 2 percent

24:14.446 --> 24:15.886 align:middle
in a manner that threatened

24:16.706 --> 24:19.246 align:middle
to move longer-term inflation
expectations materially

24:19.246 --> 24:22.146 align:middle
above 2 percent, we would use
our tools to bring April 28,

24:22.146 --> 24:24.486 align:middle
2021 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 14

24:24.486 --> 24:25.696 align:middle
of 29 inflation and expectations

24:25.696 --> 24:26.516 align:middle
down to mandate-consistent
levels.

24:26.516 --> 24:27.086 align:middle
And I would say, if I may,

24:27.086 --> 24:30.226 align:middle
that is a principal difference
from-we're all very familiar

24:30.226 --> 24:33.246 align:middle
at the Fed with the history of
the 1960s and '70s, of course.

24:33.766 --> 24:36.006 align:middle
And we know that our job is

24:36.006 --> 24:38.676 align:middle
to achieve 2 percent
inflation over time.

24:38.866 --> 24:41.946 align:middle
We're committed to that, and we
will use our tools to do that.

24:41.946 --> 24:44.826 align:middle
So that's a very different
situation than you had back

24:44.826 --> 24:48.076 align:middle
in the 1960s-or many, many
differences, actually.

24:48.556 --> 24:53.826 align:middle
So let me talk quickly
about the two reasons-two,

24:53.826 --> 24:55.026 align:middle
or you could say three,

24:55.026 --> 24:57.716 align:middle
but really two main reasons why
we think inflation will move

24:57.716 --> 24:58.446 align:middle
up in the near term.

24:58.446 --> 24:59.756 align:middle
The first is the base effects.

25:00.416 --> 25:02.656 align:middle
Twelve-month measures
of inflation are likely

25:02.656 --> 25:05.576 align:middle
to move well above 2 percent
over the next few months

25:05.576 --> 25:08.246 align:middle
as the very low inflation
readings recorded in March

25:08.246 --> 25:10.466 align:middle
and April of last year drop
out of the calculation.

25:10.836 --> 25:12.646 align:middle
That process has already
started to show up.

25:12.646 --> 25:14.226 align:middle
You saw it in the
March CPI reading,

25:14.456 --> 25:17.376 align:middle
and you'll see it later this
week in the PCE price data.

25:17.906 --> 25:19.556 align:middle
These base effects
will contribute

25:19.556 --> 25:22.886 align:middle
about 1 percentage point
to headline inflation

25:22.886 --> 25:24.846 align:middle
and about 0.7 percentage point

25:24.846 --> 25:27.166 align:middle
to core inflation
in April and May.

25:27.166 --> 25:30.356 align:middle
So, significant increases,
and they'll disappear

25:30.356 --> 25:31.756 align:middle
over the following months.

25:31.926 --> 25:33.086 align:middle
And they'll be transitory.

25:33.116 --> 25:34.946 align:middle
They carry no implication
for the rate

25:35.406 --> 25:36.926 align:middle
of inflation in later periods.

25:37.166 --> 25:38.776 align:middle
So that's base effects.

25:39.036 --> 25:42.036 align:middle
The other big one I would
talk about is bottlenecks.

25:42.036 --> 25:44.286 align:middle
So this is what we're
seeing in supply chains

25:44.286 --> 25:46.716 align:middle
in various industries, and
we're, we're in close touch

25:46.716 --> 25:47.886 align:middle
with all of these industries.

25:47.886 --> 25:51.906 align:middle
You know, the Fed has a network
of contacts that is unequaled

25:52.536 --> 25:55.086 align:middle
in businesses and in
nonprofits for that matter, too.

25:55.346 --> 25:56.646 align:middle
So what do we mean
by a bottleneck?

25:56.646 --> 25:59.526 align:middle
A bottleneck really is
a temporary blockage

25:59.526 --> 26:02.146 align:middle
or restriction in the supply
chain for, for a particular good

26:02.146 --> 26:04.836 align:middle
or goods, something that
slows down the process

26:04.836 --> 26:06.786 align:middle
of producing goods and
delivering them to the market.

26:07.216 --> 26:09.766 align:middle
We think of bottlenecks as
things that, in their nature,

26:09.766 --> 26:13.166 align:middle
will be resolved as workers and
businesses adapt, and we think

26:13.166 --> 26:15.586 align:middle
of them as not calling for
a change in monetary policy

26:15.586 --> 26:17.086 align:middle
since they're temporary
and April 28,

26:17.086 --> 26:18.936 align:middle
2021 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 15

26:18.936 --> 26:20.486 align:middle
of 29 expected to
resolve themselves.

26:20.486 --> 26:21.976 align:middle
We know that the base effects
will disappear in a few months.

26:21.976 --> 26:24.556 align:middle
It's much harder to predict
with confidence the amount

26:24.556 --> 26:26.416 align:middle
of time it will take to
resolve the bottlenecks,

26:26.876 --> 26:28.846 align:middle
or for that matter,
the temporary effects

26:29.336 --> 26:33.386 align:middle
that they will have on
prices in the meantime.

26:34.386 --> 26:37.916 align:middle
So, you know, I'll just sum up
and say, we understand our job.

26:37.916 --> 26:40.216 align:middle
We will do our job.

26:40.216 --> 26:44.376 align:middle
And we are, we are focused-as
you've seen, for many years,

26:44.376 --> 26:48.086 align:middle
we've been focused on inflation
deviating below 2 percent,

26:48.236 --> 26:49.756 align:middle
and we used our tools
aggressively

26:49.756 --> 26:51.466 align:middle
to keep it back up at 2 percent.

26:52.416 --> 26:55.746 align:middle
If, if we see inflation moving
materially above 2 percent

26:55.746 --> 27:00.176 align:middle
in a persistent way that risks
inflation expectations drifting

27:00.176 --> 27:04.556 align:middle
up, then we will use our
tools to guide inflation

27:04.556 --> 27:06.426 align:middle
and expectations back
down to 2 percent.

27:06.496 --> 27:07.906 align:middle
No one should doubt
that we will do that.

27:08.026 --> 27:09.376 align:middle
This is not what we expect.

27:10.226 --> 27:11.836 align:middle
But no one should doubt
that, in the event,

27:11.966 --> 27:13.486 align:middle
we would be prepared
to use our tools.

27:13.746 --> 27:14.086 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:14.816 --> 27:15.546 align:middle
Thank you.

27:15.666 --> 27:17.366 align:middle
James Politi.

27:17.476 --> 27:18.526 align:middle
JAMES POLITI.

27:23.326 --> 27:24.916 align:middle
Chair Powell, you
said a few weeks ago

27:24.916 --> 27:28.786 align:middle
that it would-thank
you, Chair Powell.

27:28.936 --> 27:32.026 align:middle
You said a few weeks ago that
it would take a string of months

27:32.026 --> 27:34.156 align:middle
of job creation of
about a million

27:34.156 --> 27:36.876 align:middle
to achieve progress
towards your goal.

27:37.206 --> 27:40.996 align:middle
Can you define what your, what
your definition of "a string

27:40.996 --> 27:42.856 align:middle
of months" is more specifically?

27:43.186 --> 27:46.466 align:middle
And, on inflation,
the FOMC said the rise

27:46.466 --> 27:47.626 align:middle
in inflation we're beginning

27:47.626 --> 27:50.466 align:middle
to see largely reflects
transitory factors,

27:50.466 --> 27:51.546 align:middle
as you just described.

27:51.546 --> 27:53.706 align:middle
But why use the word "largely,"

27:53.706 --> 27:56.376 align:middle
and what are the factors
driving higher prices

27:56.376 --> 27:58.136 align:middle
that may not be transitory,

27:58.486 --> 28:00.316 align:middle
based on the initial
data that you're seeing?

28:01.086 --> 28:01.356 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:01.356 --> 28:02.386 align:middle
So, what is "a string"?

28:02.386 --> 28:03.286 align:middle
What do I mean by "a string"?

28:03.286 --> 28:06.996 align:middle
Well, I would say, what we have
right now is one really good-I

28:06.996 --> 28:07.796 align:middle
can tell you what it's not.

28:07.796 --> 28:10.346 align:middle
It's not one really
good employment reading,

28:10.346 --> 28:11.516 align:middle
which is what we got in March.

28:11.516 --> 28:14.766 align:middle
We got close to a
million jobs in March

28:14.796 --> 28:17.176 align:middle
and a very strong
labor market reading.

28:17.176 --> 28:18.856 align:middle
And I was just suggesting that
we'd want to see April 28,

28:18.856 --> 28:21.276 align:middle
2021 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 16

28:21.276 --> 28:21.936 align:middle
of 29 more like that.

28:21.936 --> 28:24.006 align:middle
We're 8½ million jobs below
where we were in February

28:24.006 --> 28:26.486 align:middle
of 2020, and that
doesn't account for growth

28:26.486 --> 28:28.286 align:middle
in the labor force and
growth in the economy,

28:28.586 --> 28:29.486 align:middle
that trend we were on.

28:29.486 --> 28:32.446 align:middle
So we have-we're a long
way from our, our goals.

28:32.866 --> 28:35.316 align:middle
And we don't have to get
all the way to our goals

28:35.376 --> 28:37.016 align:middle
to taper asset purchases,
we just need

28:37.016 --> 28:38.836 align:middle
to make substantial
further progress.

28:38.916 --> 28:41.396 align:middle
It's going to take some time.

28:42.096 --> 28:45.726 align:middle
On, on "largely," there
are a bunch of factors.

28:45.726 --> 28:49.876 align:middle
I was really thinking-we were
thinking of the base effects

28:49.876 --> 28:51.256 align:middle
and also the energy effects.

28:51.256 --> 28:53.386 align:middle
I wasn't meaning to say
there are some real effects.

28:53.386 --> 28:55.986 align:middle
I mean, there are always-there
are always relative prices going

28:55.986 --> 28:57.556 align:middle
up and down within inflation.

28:57.556 --> 29:00.336 align:middle
You know, there's a
basket for CPI or PCE.

29:00.966 --> 29:03.886 align:middle
There are, you know, many, many,
many factors that go into it.

29:04.466 --> 29:06.966 align:middle
Relative prices are
always moving up and down.

29:06.966 --> 29:09.706 align:middle
This, this-we think it's,
it's very fair to say

29:09.706 --> 29:11.376 align:middle
that the increases
we see and, frankly,

29:11.376 --> 29:16.276 align:middle
are about to see later this week
are largely due to base effects.

29:16.406 --> 29:19.586 align:middle
It would have been more
contentious to say "entirely due

29:19.586 --> 29:21.446 align:middle
to base effects" because
there are some things

29:21.446 --> 29:23.566 align:middle
that are always going up, and
so we just said "largely."

29:23.716 --> 29:24.206 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

29:36.076 --> 29:36.566 align:middle
[Inaudible] CATARINA SARAIVA.

29:36.566 --> 29:37.186 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

29:37.186 --> 29:39.256 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

29:39.256 --> 29:43.236 align:middle
Over the past decade, the
Fed has invested significant

29:43.236 --> 29:47.796 align:middle
resources in large-scale bank
supervision-has completely

29:47.796 --> 29:49.386 align:middle
overhauled that approach.

29:49.386 --> 29:51.476 align:middle
And it's even created
a special committee

29:51.476 --> 29:53.296 align:middle
that looks horizontally

29:53.296 --> 29:57.386 align:middle
across the largest banks
to find common risks.

29:57.386 --> 29:59.176 align:middle
Did the Fed not see

29:59.366 --> 30:02.936 align:middle
that multiple banks had
large exposures to Archegos?

30:02.936 --> 30:05.236 align:middle
If not, why not?

30:05.236 --> 30:07.396 align:middle
And then, what regulatory
changes would you

30:07.396 --> 30:10.986 align:middle
like to see implemented to
change that going forward?

30:11.866 --> 30:12.696 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:13.116 --> 30:17.326 align:middle
We're, we, we supervise
banks to make sure

30:17.326 --> 30:20.306 align:middle
that they have riskmanagement
systems in place

30:20.306 --> 30:21.966 align:middle
so that they can
spot these things.

30:21.966 --> 30:24.366 align:middle
We don't manage their
companies for them or,

30:24.366 --> 30:26.106 align:middle
or try to manage
individual risks.

30:26.526 --> 30:28.556 align:middle
In the grand scheme of
these large April 28,

30:28.556 --> 30:30.666 align:middle
2021 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 17

30:30.666 --> 30:33.206 align:middle
of 29 institutions, the Archegos
risks were not systemically

30:33.206 --> 30:35.176 align:middle
important or were
not of the size

30:35.626 --> 30:38.016 align:middle
that they would have really
created trouble for any

30:38.016 --> 30:38.796 align:middle
of those institutions.

30:39.096 --> 30:42.536 align:middle
What was troubling, though,
was that this could happen

30:42.536 --> 30:45.726 align:middle
in a business for a number
of firms that is thought

30:45.726 --> 30:49.566 align:middle
to be-to carry relatively
well understood risks.

30:49.566 --> 30:54.276 align:middle
The prime brokerage business is,
is a well understood business,

30:54.346 --> 30:55.866 align:middle
and so it was surprising
that a number

30:55.866 --> 30:57.406 align:middle
of them would have had this.

30:57.716 --> 31:00.956 align:middle
And it was essentially,
I believe, the fact that,

31:00.986 --> 31:03.896 align:middle
that they had the same big
risk position on with a number

31:03.896 --> 31:07.046 align:middle
of firms, and they weren't-some
of the firms were not aware

31:07.046 --> 31:09.256 align:middle
that there were other firms
that had those things.

31:09.806 --> 31:11.596 align:middle
I, I wouldn't say it's
in any way an indictment

31:11.596 --> 31:13.056 align:middle
of our supervision
of these firms.

31:13.056 --> 31:16.406 align:middle
It, it-in some cases, it
may be they were-it seems

31:16.406 --> 31:18.566 align:middle
as though there were
risk-management breakdowns

31:18.566 --> 31:20.286 align:middle
at some of the firms,
not all of them.

31:20.666 --> 31:21.856 align:middle
And that's what we're
looking into.

31:22.396 --> 31:23.146 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:24.906 --> 31:25.636 align:middle
Thank you.

31:25.636 --> 31:27.496 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer.

31:27.496 --> 31:31.346 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

31:31.736 --> 31:32.636 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

31:32.856 --> 31:34.706 align:middle
I-I'm wondering,
are you planning

31:34.706 --> 31:37.626 align:middle
to visit the homeless
encampment that's near the Fed

31:37.626 --> 31:39.346 align:middle
in Washington that you drive by?

31:39.816 --> 31:41.906 align:middle
Have you been invited,
and if you went,

31:41.906 --> 31:43.716 align:middle
what would you be
looking to learn there?

31:44.086 --> 31:44.996 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:44.996 --> 31:46.116 align:middle
You know, frankly, yes.

31:46.116 --> 31:47.596 align:middle
I have not had a
chance to do it yet,

31:47.716 --> 31:49.746 align:middle
but I've been very
busy, but I will visit.

31:49.746 --> 31:51.836 align:middle
I don't want to visit
it at a time of a lot

31:51.836 --> 31:53.626 align:middle
of media attention,
because I don't want

31:53.626 --> 31:54.796 align:middle
that to be part of the story.

31:55.156 --> 31:56.776 align:middle
But I will-I will go visit when,

31:56.776 --> 31:59.876 align:middle
when it's no longer
a, a news story.

31:59.876 --> 32:05.116 align:middle
And I-you know, I-I've met with
homeless people many times,

32:05.116 --> 32:06.596 align:middle
a number of times,
anyway, let's say.

32:06.596 --> 32:09.376 align:middle
And I, I think it's always
good to talk to people

32:09.376 --> 32:10.616 align:middle
and hear what's going
on in their lives.

32:11.016 --> 32:12.686 align:middle
What you find out
is, they're you.

32:13.556 --> 32:14.386 align:middle
They're just us.

32:14.386 --> 32:18.186 align:middle
I mean, they're, they're-these
are people who, in many cases,

32:18.186 --> 32:21.266 align:middle
had jobs and, you know, they
have lives, and they've just,

32:21.326 --> 32:23.066 align:middle
they've just found
themselves in this place.

32:23.066 --> 32:24.446 align:middle
It's, it's a-it's a
difficult problem, April 28,

32:24.446 --> 32:26.586 align:middle
2021 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 18

32:26.586 --> 32:27.016 align:middle
of 29 though.

32:27.016 --> 32:28.526 align:middle
You know, there are many, many
facets of it, and I'm well aware

32:28.526 --> 32:31.076 align:middle
that this is not something that
the Fed has all the tools for

32:31.076 --> 32:31.796 align:middle
or anything like that.

32:31.856 --> 32:35.376 align:middle
But I, I will do that when,
when the need arises and when,

32:35.426 --> 32:39.226 align:middle
when it's not so much, you
know, in the public eye.

32:39.226 --> 32:40.486 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

32:40.486 --> 32:42.376 align:middle
And is there anything
specifically

32:42.376 --> 32:44.856 align:middle
that you would be
looking to learn there?

32:45.886 --> 32:48.936 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:49.116 --> 32:49.746 align:middle
Not really.

32:49.746 --> 32:51.886 align:middle
I mean, I, I think I know
what I'll find there.

32:51.916 --> 32:54.766 align:middle
I, I think you, you connect with
these people and what you-again,

32:54.766 --> 32:56.406 align:middle
what you find is,
they're like you.

32:57.006 --> 32:57.636 align:middle
That could be you.

32:57.636 --> 32:58.976 align:middle
I mean, that could
be your sister.

32:58.976 --> 33:00.606 align:middle
That could be, you
know, your kid.

33:01.506 --> 33:05.096 align:middle
You, you always feel that way in
that sort of an encounter, and,

33:05.096 --> 33:08.236 align:middle
you know, it just is a-it's
a-it's an important thing

33:08.236 --> 33:09.246 align:middle
to engage in, I think.

33:09.246 --> 33:12.116 align:middle
And I think, you know, we
bring that understanding

33:12.116 --> 33:13.246 align:middle
into our lives and, frankly,

33:13.246 --> 33:15.476 align:middle
into our work-the work
that we do as well.

33:15.646 --> 33:16.106 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:18.436 --> 33:18.956 align:middle
Thank you.

33:18.956 --> 33:19.236 align:middle
Hannah Lang.

33:19.236 --> 33:20.026 align:middle
HANNAH LANG.

33:26.486 --> 33:29.406 align:middle
Hi. I wanted to ask
about digital currency.

33:29.526 --> 33:32.566 align:middle
You previously said a few times
that you think it's important

33:32.566 --> 33:36.026 align:middle
for the U.S. to get a central
bank digital currency right

33:36.026 --> 33:37.176 align:middle
rather than be first.

33:37.176 --> 33:41.696 align:middle
But with countries like China
moving very quickly on CBDC,

33:41.696 --> 33:44.696 align:middle
I was wondering what you
think the risks are of,

33:44.826 --> 33:48.126 align:middle
of moving too slow on,
on digital currency.

33:49.656 --> 33:50.056 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:50.056 --> 33:53.696 align:middle
Right. So I think the
first thing to say is

33:53.696 --> 33:58.736 align:middle
that we feel an obligation
to understand the technology

33:58.736 --> 34:01.646 align:middle
and all of the policy
issues very, very well.

34:02.246 --> 34:05.786 align:middle
Central bank digital
currencies are now possible,

34:06.506 --> 34:09.486 align:middle
and we're going to see some
of them around the world.

34:10.676 --> 34:13.096 align:middle
And we need to understand
whether that's something

34:13.096 --> 34:15.766 align:middle
that would be a good thing
for the people that we serve.

34:16.356 --> 34:17.886 align:middle
How would it work in our system?

34:18.756 --> 34:21.726 align:middle
And there are some very, very
difficult questions to answer,

34:21.726 --> 34:26.586 align:middle
but I think we-and we are
engaged in a serious program

34:26.586 --> 34:28.426 align:middle
to understand both
the technology

34:28.486 --> 34:29.606 align:middle
and the policy issues.

34:29.606 --> 34:32.356 align:middle
April 28, 2021 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 19

34:32.356 --> 34:33.626 align:middle
of 29 I am-I would say this:

34:33.926 --> 34:37.626 align:middle
We're the world's reserve
currency, and that means

34:37.666 --> 34:40.266 align:middle
that the dollar is used
in transactions all

34:40.266 --> 34:42.376 align:middle
around the world, far more
than any other currency.

34:42.856 --> 34:46.636 align:middle
And that's because
of our rule of law;

34:46.986 --> 34:49.556 align:middle
our democratic institutions,
which are the best in the world;

34:50.066 --> 34:53.306 align:middle
our economy; our industrious
people; all the things

34:53.306 --> 34:54.746 align:middle
that make the United
States the United States.

34:54.746 --> 34:57.376 align:middle
That's why we're the,
the reserve currency.

34:57.376 --> 34:58.976 align:middle
And, of course, we have
open capital accounts,

34:58.976 --> 35:00.206 align:middle
which is essential
if you're going

35:00.206 --> 35:01.276 align:middle
to be the reserve currency.

35:02.156 --> 35:05.416 align:middle
So those are the factors that
make us the reserve currency.

35:05.646 --> 35:07.826 align:middle
I am-I'm less concerned

35:07.826 --> 35:11.686 align:middle
that someone-that another
country might have a digital

35:11.686 --> 35:12.386 align:middle
currency first.

35:12.386 --> 35:14.426 align:middle
You know, ask yourself the
question, does that mean that,

35:14.426 --> 35:16.486 align:middle
if you were a company that's
doing international business,

35:17.106 --> 35:19.556 align:middle
would you then suddenly
start to use that currency

35:19.676 --> 35:22.036 align:middle
to use your-do your
international transactions,

35:22.086 --> 35:24.416 align:middle
or would you still do
them in dollars, where,

35:24.416 --> 35:26.436 align:middle
where- which is what
everyone does?

35:26.996 --> 35:29.316 align:middle
And I'm not so concerned
about, about that.

35:29.316 --> 35:32.226 align:middle
I am really concerned
about getting it right.

35:32.716 --> 35:38.106 align:middle
It is-it is a-it is a tricky
set of questions that we have

35:38.106 --> 35:41.126 align:middle
to navigate in a world where
we already have, remember,

35:41.126 --> 35:42.736 align:middle
a highly evolved payment system.

35:43.306 --> 35:46.286 align:middle
We have FedNow and other
immediately available funds.

35:46.466 --> 35:50.096 align:middle
Pretty soon everybody will be
able to, to do what people do

35:50.096 --> 35:52.226 align:middle
in other parts of the world,
which is just use their phone

35:52.226 --> 35:55.526 align:middle
to make immediately available
payments all the time.

35:55.526 --> 35:56.696 align:middle
It'll be normal.

35:57.286 --> 36:00.006 align:middle
And what would be the role of
a central bank digital currency

36:00.106 --> 36:02.326 align:middle
in that kind of an environment?

36:02.856 --> 36:07.486 align:middle
Far more important to get it
right than it is to do it fast

36:07.486 --> 36:10.696 align:middle
or feel that we need to
rush to reach conclusions

36:10.766 --> 36:12.496 align:middle
because other countries
are moving ahead.

36:12.736 --> 36:15.716 align:middle
I mean, that-what the China-the
currency that's being used

36:15.716 --> 36:18.536 align:middle
in China is not one that
would-that would work here.

36:18.536 --> 36:22.366 align:middle
It's, it's one that really
allows the government

36:22.366 --> 36:24.226 align:middle
to see every payment
that's used-for

36:24.226 --> 36:24.796 align:middle
which it is used in real time.

36:24.796 --> 36:26.426 align:middle
It's much more to do with
things that are April 28,

36:26.426 --> 36:28.616 align:middle
2021 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 20

36:28.616 --> 36:31.346 align:middle
of 29 happening within their
own financial system than it is,

36:31.346 --> 36:33.996 align:middle
I think, to do with,
with the global-you know,

36:34.116 --> 36:35.696 align:middle
sort of global competition.

36:35.976 --> 36:36.326 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

36:38.716 --> 36:39.406 align:middle
Thank you.

36:39.406 --> 36:40.066 align:middle
Michael Derby.

36:40.066 --> 36:40.656 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

36:40.656 --> 36:42.666 align:middle
Thank you for taking
my question.

36:42.666 --> 36:44.896 align:middle
I wanted to ask you another
question about inflation,

36:44.896 --> 36:48.026 align:middle
and it has to do with
inflation expectations.

36:48.106 --> 36:50.006 align:middle
A number of different
surveys and,

36:50.006 --> 36:51.666 align:middle
and market indicators
are showing, you know,

36:51.666 --> 36:54.336 align:middle
multiyear highs in inflation
expectations ratings.

36:54.336 --> 36:57.196 align:middle
So I wondered, you know,
if, if that was something

36:57.466 --> 36:59.896 align:middle
that you saw consistent
with your inflation outlook.

36:59.896 --> 37:02.196 align:middle
Is that something that worries
you, or do you see that as,

37:02.196 --> 37:03.566 align:middle
maybe, you know, the success

37:03.716 --> 37:06.046 align:middle
of the Fed's new inflation
framework, you know,

37:06.046 --> 37:08.046 align:middle
making it out to the,
to the broader public?

37:09.266 --> 37:09.736 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:09.886 --> 37:15.486 align:middle
Right. Inflation expectations
before the pandemic hit-and here

37:15.486 --> 37:16.926 align:middle
we think of survey,

37:17.406 --> 37:21.276 align:middle
market based-survey being
either households or,

37:21.276 --> 37:23.676 align:middle
or economists and,
and market based.

37:23.676 --> 37:26.206 align:middle
You look at all of those, and
I would say that they were

37:26.466 --> 37:29.336 align:middle
at the low end of what
would have been consistent

37:29.336 --> 37:30.936 align:middle
with a 2 percent
inflation target,

37:30.936 --> 37:33.216 align:middle
particularly our 2
percent inflation target,

37:33.216 --> 37:36.176 align:middle
which calls for 2
percent average inflation.

37:36.726 --> 37:37.966 align:middle
So they're at the low end.

37:38.296 --> 37:40.806 align:middle
Inflation expectations actually
went down a bit at the beginning

37:40.806 --> 37:43.156 align:middle
of the pandemic, and now
they've just moved back

37:43.156 --> 37:48.616 align:middle
up to levels broadly-that, that
are where they were in 2018,

37:48.616 --> 37:52.076 align:middle
or in some cases 2014,
and, I would say,

37:52.076 --> 37:54.186 align:middle
more consistent with
our mandate.

37:54.676 --> 37:58.406 align:middle
And we want them to be-we want
them to be higher and-you know,

37:58.406 --> 37:59.486 align:middle
on a persistent basis.

37:59.486 --> 38:01.506 align:middle
We want inflation to
run a little bit higher

38:01.506 --> 38:03.656 align:middle
than it has been running for
the last quarter of a century.

38:03.656 --> 38:06.636 align:middle
We want it to average 2
percent, not 1.7 percent.

38:06.906 --> 38:09.076 align:middle
And for that, we need to
see inflation expectations

38:09.516 --> 38:10.736 align:middle
that are consistent with that,

38:10.736 --> 38:12.786 align:middle
really well anchored
at 2 percent.

38:13.496 --> 38:15.086 align:middle
We don't really see that yet,
but I would say that, that,

38:15.086 --> 38:16.306 align:middle
you know, breakevens
have moved up, in a way.

38:16.306 --> 38:19.356 align:middle
Breakevens April 28, 2021 Chair
Powell's Press Conference FINAL

38:19.356 --> 38:21.546 align:middle
Page 21 of 29 are based on
CPI, of course, but they're,

38:21.546 --> 38:24.316 align:middle
they're now at levels that
are-that are pretty close

38:24.316 --> 38:26.616 align:middle
to mandate consistent, whereas
before, they were below.

38:26.946 --> 38:29.296 align:middle
We monitor inflation
expectations very,

38:29.296 --> 38:31.816 align:middle
very carefully, as you
would obviously know.

38:32.376 --> 38:35.756 align:middle
So that's where-that's
where I would say it is.

38:36.886 --> 38:37.496 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

38:37.496 --> 38:38.466 align:middle
Just one small follow-up.

38:38.466 --> 38:41.966 align:middle
Do you have any redlines on
inflation readings where if,

38:41.966 --> 38:43.746 align:middle
you know, you hit, like,
a certain number on, say,

38:43.746 --> 38:46.336 align:middle
you know, core PCE,
that would be, you know,

38:46.746 --> 38:48.246 align:middle
a trigger response from the Fed?

38:48.246 --> 38:48.556 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:48.556 --> 38:50.376 align:middle
It doesn't work that way.

38:50.376 --> 38:53.956 align:middle
You know, we're, we're-inflation
measures are always going

38:53.956 --> 38:54.806 align:middle
to be a bit volatile.

38:54.806 --> 38:58.376 align:middle
We expect-as I mentioned,
we expect core and,

38:58.376 --> 39:01.636 align:middle
and headline PCE to move
up because of base effects,

39:02.196 --> 39:05.676 align:middle
and-you know, I-and we
expect that to go away.

39:05.676 --> 39:10.176 align:middle
We, we also expect these, these
bottleneck effects to come in.

39:10.176 --> 39:11.886 align:middle
We don't know how
persistent they'll be.

39:11.886 --> 39:14.976 align:middle
We, we think they'll-you
know, it's a matter of,

39:15.046 --> 39:17.116 align:middle
of when they will pass through,

39:17.116 --> 39:18.466 align:middle
not whether they
will pass through.

39:18.466 --> 39:21.106 align:middle
But we can't be confident
about the exact timing

39:21.106 --> 39:23.426 align:middle
of that or the size of them.

39:23.426 --> 39:26.186 align:middle
They don't seem especially
large at the moment, but,

39:26.186 --> 39:27.296 align:middle
you know, we, we don't know.

39:27.296 --> 39:29.086 align:middle
I mean, you know,
this is-this is all

39:29.086 --> 39:30.666 align:middle
about the reopening
of the economy.

39:30.666 --> 39:31.566 align:middle
That's what's happening.

39:31.976 --> 39:35.116 align:middle
We had it-we were in a deep,
deep hole a year ago, and now,

39:35.116 --> 39:37.986 align:middle
with a lot of help from fiscal
policy, some additional help

39:37.986 --> 39:43.066 align:middle
from monetary policy, and,
you know, a great deal of help

39:43.066 --> 39:47.046 align:middle
from vaccination, we're seeing
a strong rebound in activity.

39:47.466 --> 39:51.696 align:middle
And what's happening is, it's,
it's-demand can be spurred

39:51.926 --> 39:56.296 align:middle
with fiscal transfers and-the
saved-up fiscal transfers and,

39:56.296 --> 39:58.126 align:middle
and people going back to
work and things like that.

39:58.126 --> 40:02.406 align:middle
The supply side will take a
little bit of time to adapt.

40:02.406 --> 40:04.046 align:middle
New restaurants will
have to be opened.

40:04.596 --> 40:07.516 align:middle
The supply of various
inputs into the goods part

40:07.516 --> 40:09.686 align:middle
of the economy will have to
be brought back up to speed.

40:09.686 --> 40:10.296 align:middle
And you-you're seeing
some of that.

40:10.396 --> 40:13.396 align:middle
April 28, 2021 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 22

40:13.396 --> 40:15.076 align:middle
of 29 That'll happen
over a period

40:15.076 --> 40:16.856 align:middle
of time, over coming quarters.

40:16.856 --> 40:18.506 align:middle
You'll see some of the
bottlenecks resolved,

40:18.506 --> 40:20.766 align:middle
but the last one may not
be resolved for some time.

40:21.256 --> 40:22.576 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

40:22.626 --> 40:22.806 align:middle
Thank you.

40:22.806 --> 40:23.336 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

40:24.826 --> 40:25.706 align:middle
Thank you.

40:25.996 --> 40:26.446 align:middle
Brian Cheung.

40:26.446 --> 40:27.306 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

40:29.166 --> 40:31.036 align:middle
Brian Cheung with-hi,
Chairman Powell.

40:31.036 --> 40:32.376 align:middle
Brian Cheung with Yahoo Finance.

40:32.376 --> 40:35.256 align:middle
I wanted to ask about financial
stability, which is a part

40:35.256 --> 40:36.816 align:middle
of the Fed's reaction
function here.

40:36.996 --> 40:38.976 align:middle
It seems like to
people on the outside

40:38.976 --> 40:41.726 align:middle
who might not follow finance
daily, they're paying attention

40:41.726 --> 40:43.886 align:middle
to things like GameStop,
now Dogecoin.

40:44.116 --> 40:46.756 align:middle
And it seems like there's
interesting reach for yield

40:46.756 --> 40:48.536 align:middle
in this market-to some
extent, also Archegos.

40:48.536 --> 40:51.436 align:middle
So does the Fed see a
relationship between low rates

40:51.436 --> 40:53.136 align:middle
and easy policy to those things,

40:53.136 --> 40:55.096 align:middle
and is there a financial
stability concern

40:55.306 --> 40:56.816 align:middle
from the Fed's perspective
at this time?

40:56.816 --> 40:57.806 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:57.806 --> 40:59.686 align:middle
Right. So we look
at-financial stability

40:59.686 --> 41:03.596 align:middle
for us is really-we have a broad
framework, so we don't just jump

41:03.596 --> 41:04.566 align:middle
from one thing to another.

41:04.566 --> 41:06.906 align:middle
I know many people just look
at asset prices, and they look

41:06.906 --> 41:08.406 align:middle
at some of the things
that are going

41:08.406 --> 41:10.896 align:middle
on in the-in the
equity markets, which,

41:10.896 --> 41:13.926 align:middle
which I think do reflect
froth in the equity markets.

41:14.356 --> 41:17.116 align:middle
But really, it-really, we
try to stick to a framework

41:17.116 --> 41:19.996 align:middle
for financial stability
so we can-so we can talk

41:19.996 --> 41:21.746 align:middle
about it the same way each time

41:21.746 --> 41:23.356 align:middle
and so we can be held
accountable for it.

41:23.356 --> 41:26.376 align:middle
So one of the areas is asset
prices, and I would say some

41:26.376 --> 41:27.706 align:middle
of the asset prices are high.

41:27.996 --> 41:30.746 align:middle
You are seeing, seeing things
in the capital markets that,

41:30.746 --> 41:32.326 align:middle
that are-that are a bit frothy.

41:32.796 --> 41:33.526 align:middle
That's a fact.

41:33.926 --> 41:36.786 align:middle
I won't say it has nothing
to do with monetary policy,

41:36.786 --> 41:40.766 align:middle
but it also-it has a tremendous
amount to do with vaccination

41:40.766 --> 41:42.216 align:middle
and reopening of the economy.

41:42.216 --> 41:44.656 align:middle
That's really what has
been moving markets a lot

41:45.066 --> 41:47.696 align:middle
in the last few months
is this turn away

41:47.696 --> 41:49.766 align:middle
from what was a pretty
dark winter

41:50.236 --> 41:54.616 align:middle
to now a fast-a much
faster vaccination process

41:54.616 --> 41:55.556 align:middle
and a faster reopening.

41:55.556 --> 41:57.526 align:middle
So that's, that's part
of what's going on.

41:57.526 --> 42:00.496 align:middle
April 28, 2021 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 23

42:00.496 --> 42:02.146 align:middle
of 29 The other-the other
things, though-you know,

42:02.146 --> 42:02.956 align:middle
leverage in the financial
system,

42:02.956 --> 42:04.246 align:middle
is it- is, is not a problem.

42:04.246 --> 42:06.876 align:middle
That's a-that's a-that was
one of the four pillars.

42:06.876 --> 42:08.146 align:middle
Asset prices was one.

42:08.616 --> 42:11.896 align:middle
Leverage in the financial
system is, is not an issue;

42:11.896 --> 42:15.236 align:middle
we have very well
capitalized large banks.

42:15.236 --> 42:17.006 align:middle
We have funding risks for,

42:17.006 --> 42:18.426 align:middle
for our largest financial
institutions

42:18.426 --> 42:19.486 align:middle
that are also very low.

42:19.486 --> 42:20.986 align:middle
We do have some funding
risk issues

42:20.986 --> 42:22.146 align:middle
around money market funds,

42:22.146 --> 42:25.136 align:middle
but I would say they're
not systemic right now.

42:25.876 --> 42:28.536 align:middle
And, and the household sector is
actually in pretty good shape.

42:28.536 --> 42:31.116 align:middle
It was in-it was in
a very good shape

42:31.746 --> 42:34.976 align:middle
as a relative matter before
the financial crisis-sorry,

42:34.976 --> 42:36.576 align:middle
the pandemic crisis hit.

42:36.936 --> 42:39.276 align:middle
There were real concerns,
of course, with high levels

42:39.276 --> 42:41.366 align:middle
of unemployment and loss
of wages and all of that,

42:41.366 --> 42:44.456 align:middle
that the-that the household
sector would, would,

42:45.276 --> 42:46.416 align:middle
would weaken dramatically.

42:46.416 --> 42:47.206 align:middle
That hasn't happened.

42:47.206 --> 42:49.656 align:middle
So, with the fiscal
transfers, money that's

42:49.656 --> 42:52.116 align:middle
on household balance sheets,
they're in good shape.

42:52.116 --> 42:54.766 align:middle
You see relatively low
defaults and that kind of thing.

42:54.766 --> 42:58.756 align:middle
So the overall financial
stability picture is mixed, but,

42:58.756 --> 43:01.786 align:middle
on balance, it's-you know,
it's manageable, I would say.

43:01.786 --> 43:04.596 align:middle
And it's-by the way, I think
it's appropriate and important

43:04.946 --> 43:07.146 align:middle
for financial conditions
to remain accommodative

43:07.146 --> 43:08.866 align:middle
to support economic activity.

43:09.316 --> 43:12.546 align:middle
Again, 8½ million
people who had jobs

43:12.546 --> 43:15.686 align:middle
in February don't have
them now, and, you know,

43:15.686 --> 43:17.966 align:middle
there's a long way to go
until we reach our goal.

43:18.666 --> 43:19.956 align:middle
So that's what I would say.

43:19.956 --> 43:20.356 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

43:20.696 --> 43:23.836 align:middle
As a follow-up, you mentioned
money market pressures.

43:23.836 --> 43:25.906 align:middle
The Fed didn't make
changes to interest

43:25.906 --> 43:27.466 align:middle
on reserves or excess reserves.

43:27.466 --> 43:28.546 align:middle
What was the logic behind that?

43:28.546 --> 43:31.216 align:middle
It seems like SOFR has been
drifting closer to zero.

43:31.216 --> 43:32.806 align:middle
So just hoping for
clarification on that.

43:33.186 --> 43:33.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:33.806 --> 43:36.756 align:middle
Right. So the federal
funds rate has been well

43:36.756 --> 43:39.646 align:middle
within target and-at
target range,

43:39.646 --> 43:42.806 align:middle
and money market funds-money
market conditions are, are fine.

43:42.806 --> 43:45.876 align:middle
We have the ability to use
our administrative tools

43:45.876 --> 43:47.666 align:middle
to make sure that
that remains the case.

43:48.166 --> 43:49.536 align:middle
We do expect April 28,

43:49.536 --> 43:52.086 align:middle
2021 Chair Powell's Press
Conference FINAL Page 24

43:52.086 --> 43:53.126 align:middle
of 29 further downward
pressure on,

43:53.126 --> 43:55.916 align:middle
on rates through asset
purchases and also the runoff

43:56.006 --> 43:58.146 align:middle
in the-in the Treasury
General Account.

43:58.186 --> 44:00.706 align:middle
But, at this point, we
didn't see a need for-to,

44:00.706 --> 44:03.896 align:middle
to deploy our tools
to, to support rates.

44:03.896 --> 44:06.946 align:middle
And, of course, we will do so
if, if the need does arise.

44:06.996 --> 44:07.566 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

44:11.206 --> 44:12.046 align:middle
Thank you.

44:12.046 --> 44:12.886 align:middle
Jean Yung.

44:12.986 --> 44:14.636 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

44:15.936 --> 44:16.686 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

44:16.686 --> 44:19.856 align:middle
I wanted to ask a,
a similar question.

44:20.556 --> 44:23.566 align:middle
We are seeing elevated
market valuations,

44:23.786 --> 44:25.456 align:middle
and some economists
are concerned

44:25.456 --> 44:28.896 align:middle
that the economy might overheat,
at least for a period of time.

44:28.896 --> 44:31.616 align:middle
So should the Fed and other
regulators be thinking

44:31.616 --> 44:34.166 align:middle
about tightening
capital requirements

44:34.216 --> 44:36.826 align:middle
or extending oversight
to the nonbank sector

44:36.826 --> 44:39.896 align:middle
so that financial
stability risks stay

44:39.896 --> 44:41.226 align:middle
as low as they have been?

44:41.226 --> 44:41.726 align:middle
Thank you.

44:42.656 --> 44:42.866 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:42.866 --> 44:46.036 align:middle
So I think capital
requirements for banks are,

44:46.366 --> 44:52.526 align:middle
to me-went up tremendously,
really, over the course

44:52.526 --> 44:54.366 align:middle
of the 10 years between
the financial crisis

44:54.366 --> 44:55.506 align:middle
and the arrival of the pandemic.

44:56.256 --> 44:58.606 align:middle
And the banks really made it

44:58.606 --> 45:05.396 align:middle
through a real stress test
very well and passed three,

45:05.546 --> 45:07.446 align:middle
three of our-two of
our stress tests,

45:07.446 --> 45:09.906 align:middle
and that is another
one that's pending.

45:09.946 --> 45:14.136 align:middle
So capital is, is in a good
place, as far as I'm concerned,

45:14.226 --> 45:15.176 align:middle
in the banking system.

45:15.716 --> 45:20.626 align:middle
But, to your point,
what we saw was-so what,

45:20.626 --> 45:24.756 align:middle
what kind of happened during
the pandemic crisis that,

45:24.756 --> 45:29.446 align:middle
that requires attention, and
number one is money market funds

45:29.446 --> 45:34.106 align:middle
and corporate bond funds where
we saw run dynamics again,

45:34.736 --> 45:37.596 align:middle
and we need to-we're,
we're looking at that.

45:37.596 --> 45:39.206 align:middle
So we're looking at ways,

45:39.526 --> 45:41.546 align:middle
and people around the
world are looking at ways,

45:41.546 --> 45:47.626 align:middle
to make those vehicles resilient
so that they don't have to be,

45:47.776 --> 45:50.486 align:middle
you know, supported by the
government whenever there's,

45:50.766 --> 45:53.226 align:middle
you know, severely
stressed market conditions.

45:53.226 --> 45:54.186 align:middle
It's a private business.

45:54.226 --> 45:57.176 align:middle
They, they need to have the
wherewithal to stay in business

45:57.176 --> 45:59.066 align:middle
and not just count on the Fed

45:59.066 --> 46:00.906 align:middle
and others to-around
the world to come in.

46:00.906 --> 46:02.166 align:middle
So that was that.

46:02.166 --> 46:05.806 align:middle
April 28, 2021 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 25

46:05.806 --> 46:07.496 align:middle
of 29 The other one is
Treasury market structure.

46:07.496 --> 46:12.146 align:middle
Dealers are committing less
capital to that activity now

46:12.186 --> 46:14.936 align:middle
than they were 10 or 15
years ago, and the need

46:14.936 --> 46:17.266 align:middle
for capital is higher
because there's

46:17.266 --> 46:19.866 align:middle
so much more supply
of Treasuries.

46:20.656 --> 46:25.046 align:middle
And so there are some questions
about Treasury market structure,

46:25.046 --> 46:26.816 align:middle
and there's a lot of
careful work going

46:26.816 --> 46:30.626 align:middle
on to understand whether there's
something we can do about this,

46:31.426 --> 46:35.546 align:middle
because we-you know, the U.S.
Treasury market is probably the

46:35.546 --> 46:39.706 align:middle
most single important-single
most important market in,

46:40.236 --> 46:41.636 align:middle
in the economy and the world.

46:42.086 --> 46:42.966 align:middle
It needs to be liquid.

46:42.966 --> 46:45.336 align:middle
It needs to function well
for the good of our economy

46:45.656 --> 46:46.866 align:middle
and the good of our citizens.

46:46.926 --> 46:51.136 align:middle
So it's not clear what,
what-where that takes you,

46:51.136 --> 46:55.436 align:middle
but we, we are taking a careful
look-and the Treasury Department

46:55.436 --> 46:58.406 align:middle
is, is really going
to be leading this-at,

46:58.516 --> 47:02.406 align:middle
at Treasury market structure
and all of the various aspects

47:03.066 --> 47:05.706 align:middle
of that to make sure that
we do have a resilient,

47:05.706 --> 47:10.246 align:middle
strong Treasury market that can
work even in difficult times.

47:10.246 --> 47:11.756 align:middle
As, as you know, at
the very beginning

47:11.756 --> 47:14.326 align:middle
of the financial-of
the-of this recent crisis,

47:14.946 --> 47:17.706 align:middle
there was such a demand
for selling Treasuries,

47:19.096 --> 47:23.366 align:middle
including by foreign
central banks, that, really,

47:23.366 --> 47:25.006 align:middle
the dealers couldn't
handle the volume.

47:25.006 --> 47:30.696 align:middle
And so what was happening was,
the market was really starting

47:30.696 --> 47:32.136 align:middle
to lose function,

47:32.136 --> 47:35.026 align:middle
and that's-that was a really
serious problem which we had

47:35.026 --> 47:38.286 align:middle
to solve through really
massive asset purchases.

47:38.846 --> 47:41.126 align:middle
And, you know, so we'd
like to, we'd like to see

47:41.126 --> 47:45.346 align:middle
if there isn't something we can
do to-is-do we need to build

47:45.346 --> 47:47.656 align:middle
against that kind of an
extreme tail risk, and if so,

47:47.656 --> 47:48.456 align:middle
what would that look like?

47:48.886 --> 47:51.876 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

47:52.596 --> 47:53.426 align:middle
Thank you.

47:53.426 --> 47:54.276 align:middle
Edward Lawrence.

47:54.276 --> 47:54.606 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

47:55.226 --> 47:58.046 align:middle
Thank you, Fed Chairman,
for the question.

47:58.046 --> 47:59.686 align:middle
So I'm interested
in your thoughts.

47:59.686 --> 48:01.506 align:middle
There's so much fiscal
help and accommodation

48:01.506 --> 48:02.846 align:middle
from the Federal Reserve.

48:02.976 --> 48:05.566 align:middle
You said today the vaccinations
will follow the normal economic

48:05.566 --> 48:09.326 align:middle
conditions or, or lead to more
normal economic conditions later

48:09.326 --> 48:09.856 align:middle
this year.

48:10.156 --> 48:12.976 align:middle
When do you see the
economy being able to stand

48:12.976 --> 48:14.116 align:middle
on its own feet, so to speak?

48:14.116 --> 48:15.766 align:middle
And, along those lines,
with the fiscal spending

48:15.766 --> 48:18.796 align:middle
and the April 28, 2021 Chair
Powell's Press Conference FINAL

48:18.796 --> 48:20.276 align:middle
Page 26 of 29 accommodation
you talked

48:20.276 --> 48:22.236 align:middle
about with transitory inflation,
does more spending need

48:22.236 --> 48:24.436 align:middle
to be injected into the
system, or will that affect

48:24.436 --> 48:26.756 align:middle
that transitory nation-nature
of the inflation?

48:26.756 --> 48:27.176 align:middle
Thank you.

48:28.536 --> 48:28.796 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:28.796 --> 48:32.476 align:middle
When will the economy be, be
able to stand on its own feet?

48:32.476 --> 48:35.466 align:middle
I'm not sure if-I'm not
sure what the exact nature

48:35.466 --> 48:36.316 align:middle
of that question is.

48:36.466 --> 48:37.756 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

48:37.756 --> 48:39.836 align:middle
What, what I'm saying
is, is, you know,

48:39.836 --> 48:42.996 align:middle
when will the-when will
you need to lower the,

48:42.996 --> 48:44.726 align:middle
the amount of Treasuries
you're buying to sort

48:44.726 --> 48:45.676 align:middle
of taper off a little bit?

48:45.676 --> 48:48.346 align:middle
When can it stand without
having that support

48:48.346 --> 48:50.116 align:middle
from the monetary policy side?

48:50.116 --> 48:52.356 align:middle
And then-and then,
further, the transitory.

48:52.356 --> 48:52.886 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:52.886 --> 48:53.386 align:middle
Okay. Sorry.

48:53.386 --> 48:56.476 align:middle
So we have-you know, we've
articulated our test for that,

48:56.476 --> 48:59.446 align:middle
as you know, and that is just,
we'll continue asset purchases

48:59.446 --> 49:02.566 align:middle
at this pace until we see
substantial further progress.

49:03.076 --> 49:05.446 align:middle
And we're, we're going
to communicate well

49:05.446 --> 49:06.786 align:middle
in advance of any decision.

49:06.786 --> 49:10.226 align:middle
We're going to let, you
know, the, the public know

49:10.356 --> 49:11.366 align:middle
that that's what we're thinking.

49:11.366 --> 49:13.606 align:middle
And, and so there'll be a lot of
warning and that kind of thing.

49:13.606 --> 49:15.426 align:middle
But it's about substantial
further progress toward

49:15.426 --> 49:15.946 align:middle
our goals.

49:15.946 --> 49:17.386 align:middle
That's, that's really all it is.

49:17.386 --> 49:19.016 align:middle
It doesn't have any,
any external,

49:19.576 --> 49:22.186 align:middle
virusrelated specific
requirement, although, again,

49:22.186 --> 49:25.916 align:middle
I do think it'll-you know, the,
the virus will need to continue

49:26.026 --> 49:29.146 align:middle
to be controlled for us to
achieve those economic goals.

49:29.146 --> 49:30.486 align:middle
But it's really the
economic goals.

49:31.036 --> 49:32.426 align:middle
Now, your second question.

49:32.426 --> 49:33.676 align:middle
Sorry, just say again.

49:33.676 --> 49:35.236 align:middle
I didn't quite get
that question.

49:35.766 --> 49:36.456 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

49:36.456 --> 49:36.646 align:middle
Yes, sure.

49:36.646 --> 49:39.006 align:middle
With all the fiscal
spending that's happened

49:39.006 --> 49:40.606 align:middle
and the accommodation and-does,

49:40.606 --> 49:43.266 align:middle
does the system need
more spending either

49:43.266 --> 49:44.856 align:middle
from the fiscal side
or accommodation,

49:44.856 --> 49:47.986 align:middle
or would more spending
affect the transitory nature

49:48.216 --> 49:51.566 align:middle
of the inflation and put
those upward, upward pressures

49:51.566 --> 49:52.476 align:middle
on inflation long term?

49:52.476 --> 49:55.456 align:middle
April 28, 2021 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 27

49:55.456 --> 49:56.576 align:middle
of 29 CHAIR POWELL.

49:56.576 --> 49:58.796 align:middle
So it's really up to Congress.

49:58.796 --> 50:00.106 align:middle
We-we're not an adviser
to Congress.

50:00.106 --> 50:01.446 align:middle
You know, we don't,
we don't weigh

50:01.446 --> 50:04.146 align:middle
in on specific fiscal
bills or proposals.

50:04.146 --> 50:07.916 align:middle
That's really between
elected parties.

50:08.126 --> 50:11.416 align:middle
You know, basically, fiscal
policy is the province of people

50:11.416 --> 50:16.256 align:middle
who stand for democratic
election and win, and they get

50:16.256 --> 50:18.176 align:middle
to make those very difficult
decisions, and they get

50:18.176 --> 50:19.316 align:middle
to be accountable to the voters.

50:19.316 --> 50:22.716 align:middle
We didn't do any of that, and we
don't have a seat at that table.

50:22.716 --> 50:24.506 align:middle
We don't seek a seat
at that table.

50:24.506 --> 50:26.916 align:middle
So that's really
something for Congress

50:26.916 --> 50:28.966 align:middle
and the Administration
to, to deal with.

50:30.156 --> 50:30.916 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

50:31.496 --> 50:32.146 align:middle
Thank you.

50:32.146 --> 50:32.946 align:middle
Greg Robb.

50:37.396 --> 50:37.946 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

50:37.946 --> 50:39.606 align:middle
Hi, Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

50:39.606 --> 50:41.876 align:middle
Thank you-thank you so much
for the-for the opportunity.

50:41.876 --> 50:44.946 align:middle
I wanted to circle back
to the housing market.

50:44.946 --> 50:48.436 align:middle
It's just kind of
confusing that the question

50:48.436 --> 50:49.356 align:middle
and your answer-you know,

50:49.356 --> 50:51.736 align:middle
the housing market is
strong, prices are up.

50:51.866 --> 50:56.126 align:middle
And yet the Fed is buying
$40 billion per month

50:56.126 --> 50:57.606 align:middle
in mortgage-related assets.

50:58.146 --> 51:01.636 align:middle
Why is that, and are those
purchases playing a role

51:01.636 --> 51:03.436 align:middle
at all in pushing up prices?

51:03.706 --> 51:05.656 align:middle
Thank you.

51:05.656 --> 51:05.866 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

51:05.866 --> 51:08.686 align:middle
Yes. I, I mean, we're-we
started buying MBS

51:08.876 --> 51:12.226 align:middle
because the mortgage-backed
security market was,

51:12.226 --> 51:16.566 align:middle
was really experiencing severe
dysfunction, and we've sort of,

51:16.566 --> 51:21.216 align:middle
sort of articulated, you know,
what our exit path is from that.

51:21.696 --> 51:25.266 align:middle
It's not meant to provide
direct assistance to,

51:25.506 --> 51:26.556 align:middle
to the housing market.

51:26.556 --> 51:27.516 align:middle
That was never the intent.

51:27.516 --> 51:32.316 align:middle
It was really just to keep that
as-it's a very close relation

51:32.316 --> 51:33.466 align:middle
to the Treasury market

51:33.906 --> 51:35.916 align:middle
and a very important
market on its own.

51:36.016 --> 51:37.496 align:middle
And so that's, that's why we,

51:37.576 --> 51:39.796 align:middle
we bought as we did during
the Global Financial Crisis;

51:39.796 --> 51:41.036 align:middle
we bought MBS too.

51:41.896 --> 51:46.306 align:middle
Again, not, not an intention to
send help to the housing market,

51:46.306 --> 51:51.156 align:middle
which was, which was really not,
not a problem this time at all.

51:51.256 --> 51:55.456 align:middle
So-and, you know, it's, it's
a situation where we will,

51:55.456 --> 51:59.066 align:middle
we will taper asset purchases
when the time comes to do that,

51:59.066 --> 52:01.126 align:middle
and those, those purchases
will come to zero over time.

52:01.126 --> 52:01.656 align:middle
And that time is not yet.

52:01.656 --> 52:05.336 align:middle
April 28, 2021 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 28

52:05.336 --> 52:06.986 align:middle
of 29 GREG ROBB.

52:06.986 --> 52:07.166 align:middle
Thank you.

52:07.166 --> 52:07.566 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

52:07.566 --> 52:07.786 align:middle
Thank you.

52:07.786 --> 52:09.166 align:middle
And for the last question,
we'll go to Mike McKee.

52:09.166 --> 52:09.526 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

52:11.766 --> 52:13.166 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

52:13.256 --> 52:16.436 align:middle
Since I am last, let me go
back to Paul's question,

52:16.436 --> 52:21.566 align:middle
the first question, and ask
him-ask you whether-not whether

52:21.566 --> 52:24.446 align:middle
you're thinking about thinking
of tapering, but why you're not.

52:25.016 --> 52:26.636 align:middle
We're seeing bank lending fall.

52:27.036 --> 52:29.326 align:middle
The markets seem to
be operating well.

52:29.756 --> 52:32.426 align:middle
Are you afraid of
a taper tantrum?

52:32.426 --> 52:34.316 align:middle
Or is it, as one
money manager put it,

52:34.746 --> 52:36.846 align:middle
"If you get out of the markets,
there aren't enough buyers

52:36.846 --> 52:38.166 align:middle
for all of the Treasury debt,

52:38.656 --> 52:41.626 align:middle
and so rates would
have to go way up"?

52:41.676 --> 52:43.596 align:middle
The bottom-line question
is, what do we get

52:43.596 --> 52:46.836 align:middle
for $120 billion a month that
we couldn't get for less?

52:46.836 --> 52:47.936 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

52:48.166 --> 52:50.466 align:middle
So it's really not more
complicated than this.

52:50.466 --> 52:52.556 align:middle
We, we articulated the

52:52.556 --> 52:55.926 align:middle
"substantial further progress"
test at our December meeting.

52:56.556 --> 52:58.656 align:middle
And, really, for the
next couple of months,

52:58.656 --> 53:02.596 align:middle
we made relatively little
progress toward our goals.

53:02.596 --> 53:05.186 align:middle
And, remember, substantial
further progress

53:05.186 --> 53:06.966 align:middle
from December-from
our December meeting.

53:07.496 --> 53:12.356 align:middle
And then vaccination started
to get more widespread.

53:13.096 --> 53:14.316 align:middle
The economy reopened.

53:14.476 --> 53:16.596 align:middle
We got a really nice
job report for March.

53:16.856 --> 53:20.146 align:middle
It doesn't constitute
substantial further progress.

53:20.686 --> 53:22.546 align:middle
It's not close to
substantial further progress.

53:22.546 --> 53:28.046 align:middle
We're hopeful that we will
see along this path a way

53:28.046 --> 53:28.836 align:middle
to that goal.

53:28.836 --> 53:31.076 align:middle
And we believe we will; it
just is a question of when.

53:31.676 --> 53:35.086 align:middle
And so, when that time-when
the time comes for us to talk

53:35.086 --> 53:36.496 align:middle
about talking about
it, we'll do that.

53:37.226 --> 53:38.726 align:middle
And-but that time is not now.

53:38.806 --> 53:40.426 align:middle
It's-we're just not-we're
not that far.

53:40.426 --> 53:42.286 align:middle
We've had one great jobs report.

53:42.536 --> 53:43.196 align:middle
It's not enough.

53:43.196 --> 53:43.856 align:middle
You know, we're going to act

53:43.856 --> 53:45.146 align:middle
on actual data, not
on our forecast.

53:45.146 --> 53:46.266 align:middle
And we're just going to
need to see more data.

53:46.266 --> 53:47.866 align:middle
That's-it's no, no more
complicated than that.

53:47.866 --> 53:50.476 align:middle
April 28, 2021 Chair Powell's
Press Conference FINAL Page 29

53:50.476 --> 53:51.086 align:middle
of 29 MICHAEL MCKEE.

53:51.086 --> 53:54.076 align:middle
Well, if you leave
rates where they are,

53:54.076 --> 53:55.916 align:middle
it doesn't change anything.

53:55.916 --> 53:59.566 align:middle
But does it change anything
if you actually tapered a bit?

53:59.606 --> 54:02.556 align:middle
If you spent less, would you
still get the same effect

54:02.556 --> 54:03.196 align:middle
on the economy?

54:03.566 --> 54:04.266 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

54:04.466 --> 54:05.626 align:middle
If we bought less?

54:05.626 --> 54:06.856 align:middle
You're, you're very faint.

54:06.856 --> 54:08.836 align:middle
So if someone has the
volume, they can turn it up.

54:08.836 --> 54:10.316 align:middle
But if we bought
less, you know, no.

54:10.316 --> 54:12.356 align:middle
I mean, I think the
effect is proportional

54:12.356 --> 54:13.276 align:middle
to the amount we buy.

54:13.736 --> 54:15.826 align:middle
It's, it's really part

54:15.826 --> 54:18.736 align:middle
of overall accommodative
financial conditions.

54:18.736 --> 54:21.446 align:middle
We, we have tried to create
accommodative financial

54:21.446 --> 54:24.866 align:middle
conditions to support economic
activity, and we did that.

54:24.866 --> 54:27.496 align:middle
And we articulated
the-you know, the,

54:27.536 --> 54:30.076 align:middle
the tests for withdrawing
that accommodation.

54:30.076 --> 54:33.286 align:middle
And we think, you know-so we're
waiting to see those tests

54:33.286 --> 54:35.666 align:middle
to be fulfilled both
for asset purchases

54:35.796 --> 54:37.226 align:middle
and for liftoff of rates.

54:38.076 --> 54:40.716 align:middle
And, you know, when the
tests are fulfilled,

54:40.716 --> 54:41.466 align:middle
we'll, we'll go ahead.

54:41.466 --> 54:42.596 align:middle
As we-you know, we've
done this before.

54:42.596 --> 54:46.976 align:middle
We did it in, in the last-after
the last crisis, and, you know,

54:46.976 --> 54:51.506 align:middle
we'll do it in, in maybe-we'll
do it-as those tests are

54:51.506 --> 54:52.606 align:middle
satisfied, we'll do it.

54:52.926 --> 54:54.566 align:middle
And the-and the only thing

54:54.566 --> 54:57.836 align:middle
that will guide us
is, are the tests met?

54:58.256 --> 54:59.976 align:middle
You know, that, that's what
we focused on: Is-have,

54:59.976 --> 55:01.696 align:middle
have the macroeconomic
conditions

55:01.696 --> 55:04.196 align:middle
that we've articulated,
have they been realized?

55:04.196 --> 55:08.616 align:middle
That will be the test for,
for tapering asset purchases

55:08.706 --> 55:10.236 align:middle
and for raising interest rates.

55:10.346 --> 55:10.846 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

55:14.496 --> 55:15.676 align:middle
Thank you very much.

55:16.756 --> 55:17.246 align:middle
Take care.

55:17.486 --> 55:17.946 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

55:18.096 --> 55:20.806 align:middle
Thank you.

